958-b35e-45b30741404e" :question/math-academy-id "q-28925" :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y={6}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={6}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={6}^{1}=6.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,6).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/01/016f88de4ffd29f3b5721c767bf9e2db493ef7ac91dc2613ee7f3169dcba1718.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0825d21e-185b-5d87-a42e-9c60a08961ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0825d21e-185b-5d87-a42e-9c60a08961ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "0825d21e-185b-5d87-a42e-9c60a08961ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21da3bba-8fd7-5e30-9f51-a68ed25d5f4d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21da3bba-8fd7-5e30-9f51-a68ed25d5f4d" "answer-06874964-3a0d-5dab-8f71-b70c5a63dd33" "answer-7f7be3ac-1312-5850-a3ce-3f95c73cc02d" "answer-e37b529a-b09e-524b-9655-586ba2a25497" "answer-a8906546-8a3e-5a05-8b70-662b3238db64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21da3bba-8fd7-5e30-9f51-a68ed25d5f4d" :answer/id #uuid "21da3bba-8fd7-5e30-9f51-a68ed25d5f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/01/016f88de4ffd29f3b5721c767bf9e2db493ef7ac91dc2613ee7f3169dcba1718.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06874964-3a0d-5dab-8f71-b70c5a63dd33" :answer/id #uuid "06874964-3a0d-5dab-8f71-b70c5a63dd33" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a8/a853afe945aec222c4d12f75fbb8b322706791fb44fd7e716eb4385cd44edbeb.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f7be3ac-1312-5850-a3ce-3f95c73cc02d" :answer/id #uuid "7f7be3ac-1312-5850-a3ce-3f95c73cc02d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e6/e692cef207a5c8c821dd6aeb9c3c62a0c2eaf1f348698af1de9ffa476df280b5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e37b529a-b09e-524b-9655-586ba2a25497" :answer/id #uuid "e37b529a-b09e-524b-9655-586ba2a25497" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ed/edb4884e50b6a473996efd016b1860178a83a828612da09ea90f14f01b8d27db.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8906546-8a3e-5a05-8b70-662b3238db64" :answer/id #uuid "a8906546-8a3e-5a05-8b70-662b3238db64" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ff/ff60a8e58008c0e3816324903f7c5195c8f6591c308ef05e3d93ad6411790bd9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c063995-cffa-55cf-8462-64cb05fc0c3c" :question/id #uuid "8c063995-cffa-55cf-8462-64cb05fc0c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-46059" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to +\\,\\infty $ as $x\\to +\\,\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/45/4531a531fb98f092751cec4552d6ead2bf828759842214f030618dba1ea85893.png)\n\n \n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x>0,$ the graph increases without a bound as $x$ increases to infinity.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55e7eb3d-b4ba-5f6a-8826-1bca21045615"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55e7eb3d-b4ba-5f6a-8826-1bca21045615" :answer-field/id #uuid "55e7eb3d-b4ba-5f6a-8826-1bca21045615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46a450a1-a396-59e1-9a6b-cb0e767f8fad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70bfc9bf-2885-5b6b-92ef-d910c905221b" "answer-46a450a1-a396-59e1-9a6b-cb0e767f8fad" "answer-961b0c01-83be-5cb1-9a7b-ff49a971b18e" "answer-2e229a88-7e42-58dd-a8d6-ae3390023bab" "answer-bc5675ff-f62b-5eb3-9643-02cc5d50e980"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70bfc9bf-2885-5b6b-92ef-d910c905221b" :answer/id #uuid "70bfc9bf-2885-5b6b-92ef-d910c905221b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46a450a1-a396-59e1-9a6b-cb0e767f8fad" :answer/id #uuid "46a450a1-a396-59e1-9a6b-cb0e767f8fad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-961b0c01-83be-5cb1-9a7b-ff49a971b18e" :answer/id #uuid "961b0c01-83be-5cb1-9a7b-ff49a971b18e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e229a88-7e42-58dd-a8d6-ae3390023bab" :answer/id #uuid "2e229a88-7e42-58dd-a8d6-ae3390023bab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc5675ff-f62b-5eb3-9643-02cc5d50e980" :answer/id #uuid "bc5675ff-f62b-5eb3-9643-02cc5d50e980" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-302012e2-b0bf-56ed-8145-9c936fb36ce2" :question/id #uuid "302012e2-b0bf-56ed-8145-9c936fb36ce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-47739" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={5}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $(-\\infty ,5).$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\,\\infty .$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/31/31c990964a5a04c4a44773bb00d1b097999951b823e3b10861d13e38c364b8b5.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of $f(x)={5}^{x}$ is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true, since for $x<0$, the graph gets close to zero as we decrease $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c0019f1-586d-574b-93ab-bd726d43763d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c0019f1-586d-574b-93ab-bd726d43763d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c0019f1-586d-574b-93ab-bd726d43763d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f0703391-9746-55e8-bdc7-282159419398" :answer-field/choices ["answer-622a920b-f7cc-50a7-a623-080277e44780" "answer-db1a3679-96b2-5175-9edf-4e5f2b677211" "answer-f0703391-9746-55e8-bdc7-282159419398" "answer-6a08bbff-2d42-5a8c-b055-690e677fc271" "answer-ebb7e80c-90dc-5734-81f1-382ec59c5e20"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-622a920b-f7cc-50a7-a623-080277e44780" :answer/id #uuid "622a920b-f7cc-50a7-a623-080277e44780" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db1a3679-96b2-5175-9edf-4e5f2b677211" :answer/id #uuid "db1a3679-96b2-5175-9edf-4e5f2b677211" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0703391-9746-55e8-bdc7-282159419398" :answer/id #uuid "f0703391-9746-55e8-bdc7-282159419398" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a08bbff-2d42-5a8c-b055-690e677fc271" :answer/id #uuid "6a08bbff-2d42-5a8c-b055-690e677fc271" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebb7e80c-90dc-5734-81f1-382ec59c5e20" :answer/id #uuid "ebb7e80c-90dc-5734-81f1-382ec59c5e20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f281af5-b28c-5775-bf2f-eca0f2d16ebb" :question/id #uuid "7f281af5-b28c-5775-bf2f-eca0f2d16ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-7235" :question/problem "![](images/90/90626ef73fbd9c0bd94802275961ca0c604a76f0d01faa1889b7dd3524ad6132.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({4}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k({4}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={4}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3338c64b-fdc6-5d7e-9864-167da0a1603e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3338c64b-fdc6-5d7e-9864-167da0a1603e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3338c64b-fdc6-5d7e-9864-167da0a1603e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7eb8905f-4868-5bfb-a51f-7a6bef705efb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-357d6e13-353c-5021-823b-660196a0ea45" "answer-55f3c50c-4ff4-58ee-962e-908f5dbf77e1" "answer-2fd2e8e0-9905-54e6-81a0-d7a3b82ee823" "answer-7eb8905f-4868-5bfb-a51f-7a6bef705efb" "answer-dce2766b-d402-5cd1-b80d-96bf1f25fb39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-357d6e13-353c-5021-823b-660196a0ea45" :answer/id #uuid "357d6e13-353c-5021-823b-660196a0ea45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55f3c50c-4ff4-58ee-962e-908f5dbf77e1" :answer/id #uuid "55f3c50c-4ff4-58ee-962e-908f5dbf77e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fd2e8e0-9905-54e6-81a0-d7a3b82ee823" :answer/id #uuid "2fd2e8e0-9905-54e6-81a0-d7a3b82ee823" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eb8905f-4868-5bfb-a51f-7a6bef705efb" :answer/id #uuid "7eb8905f-4868-5bfb-a51f-7a6bef705efb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dce2766b-d402-5cd1-b80d-96bf1f25fb39" :answer/id #uuid "dce2766b-d402-5cd1-b80d-96bf1f25fb39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d727472-a16c-5704-a3dd-e6b93f9d685f" :question/id #uuid "9d727472-a16c-5704-a3dd-e6b93f9d685f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7118" :question/problem "![](images/af/afeee9a0b7dd8a68368444ca475a6ed9493f0dfb007f4e8406b8fe2a32abf9e4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k({2}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={2}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,2)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d7f74135-1719-59e7-a4de-fc90530e85bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d7f74135-1719-59e7-a4de-fc90530e85bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f74135-1719-59e7-a4de-fc90530e85bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2e918a36-d84a-52b6-8a6f-fe07207b7c6b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98d88c1c-d97c-59d0-b056-2b17db6467ae" "answer-e3de98f4-1e5b-5aeb-821f-b3abb97bccf1" "answer-2e918a36-d84a-52b6-8a6f-fe07207b7c6b" "answer-0fb64125-a7d3-5b49-a2f6-4235214e697f" "answer-4602128f-5d65-569b-8d8a-ca3d5b50a532"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98d88c1c-d97c-59d0-b056-2b17db6467ae" :answer/id #uuid "98d88c1c-d97c-59d0-b056-2b17db6467ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3de98f4-1e5b-5aeb-821f-b3abb97bccf1" :answer/id #uuid "e3de98f4-1e5b-5aeb-821f-b3abb97bccf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e918a36-d84a-52b6-8a6f-fe07207b7c6b" :answer/id #uuid "2e918a36-d84a-52b6-8a6f-fe07207b7c6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fb64125-a7d3-5b49-a2f6-4235214e697f" :answer/id #uuid "0fb64125-a7d3-5b49-a2f6-4235214e697f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4602128f-5d65-569b-8d8a-ca3d5b50a532" :answer/id #uuid "4602128f-5d65-569b-8d8a-ca3d5b50a532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110930 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $(\\frac{2}{7},\\infty ).$\n\nII. Its domain is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(1,\\frac{3}{7}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/52/5259fcd21c4fe03e4facb4e8a49860122e051b8427e4342a829f150aa3b30e6e.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. $f(1)=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{7}.$ Therefore, the point $(1,\\frac{3}{7})$ lies on the curve.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107471 :answer-field/correct 17592186107475 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111100 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the graph of $y=0.5({9}^{x})?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={9}^{x}$:\n\n![](images/27/270006d7d2e7d7cab172ba586df796af8b78d9b00157f439bf65cc0e151ed13e.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph vertically by a factor of $0.5.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,0.5),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3d0e0db5029b92622d28c478c6a8d3ee02dee21fe3fa1f82965838b22d07f543.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186108340 :answer-field/correct 17592186108343 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111825 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x.$ First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1).$\n\n2\\. The graph is increasing.\n\n3\\. For $x>0,$ the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\n4\\. For $x<0,$ the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n5\\. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty , \\infty )$, and the range is $y>0.$\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1.$ These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111826 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/68/68dcb337cb0927d43403eebeb21c0a54dc46a136f7cd21ce821d9bfc325c748e.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)  It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/70/7052037576cfaf29807f0ad332100b9929a72806846219da2599c8f911500d87.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111833 :tutorial/content "The curve ![](images/e9/e910a01694746b83e1662e73779d7e758399e2d0bdf7441bbd8221a37f278b17.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) This has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/68/685e360ed7bcd7975aa705d192ef487af17688bb113b20e10f881a0bdf7f46b7.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/97/97a51f546ed69cb656e8a811f3a4acf3f6dacce8e9ed7cac63419ad63f0497d1.svg)\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110875 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-02669dc0-8a6e-5ef4-aa36-77e644af949e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186365934 :answer-field/correct "answer-0c1f60b5-ed12-5102-ae8c-d5d79e17c1db" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c1f60b5-ed12-5102-ae8c-d5d79e17c1db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c1f60b5-ed12-5102-ae8c-d5d79e17c1db" :answer/id #uuid "0c1f60b5-ed12-5102-ae8c-d5d79e17c1db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-e^{-3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186365937 :answer-field/correct "answer-e6e484f7-c12f-51da-8084-5c596183b805" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6e484f7-c12f-51da-8084-5c596183b805"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6e484f7-c12f-51da-8084-5c596183b805" :answer/id #uuid "e6e484f7-c12f-51da-8084-5c596183b805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5e^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-02669dc0-8a6e-5ef4-aa36-77e644af949e" :question/id #uuid "02669dc0-8a6e-5ef4-aa36-77e644af949e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343799" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=f(x),\\,y(4)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(4)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+2y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+2y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(4)=3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4) & =A{e}^{-2(4)}+{y}_{p}(4) \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =3{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(3{e}^{8}){e}^{-2x}=3{e}^{8-2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼3{e}^{8-2x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d219bdc7-f555-5794-9513-0ba34124b55e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d219bdc7-f555-5794-9513-0ba34124b55e" :answer-field/id #uuid "d219bdc7-f555-5794-9513-0ba34124b55e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f6cc1010-4fa1-546b-948e-840f04280c2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6cc1010-4fa1-546b-948e-840f04280c2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6cc1010-4fa1-546b-948e-840f04280c2b" :answer/id #uuid "f6cc1010-4fa1-546b-948e-840f04280c2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3e^{8-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-07d6e91f-8b7b-5563-b227-9803067c1e75" :activity/id #uuid "07d6e91f-8b7b-5563-b227-9803067c1e75" :activity/title "Solving IVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049479 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-d35c11bd-f0d7-595a-b549-5b8b757eba5d" "step-57c40029-7a9d-590e-8482-18a486295420" "step-62326fde-cea3-5a99-a3e3-e672607ec1fa" "step-d7ac7bce-1bb6-5197-aa1a-34fb4ea4e07e" "step-5fca549c-1acc-5d66-bb4d-25ca00cb6c5e"] :activity/first-step "step-d35c11bd-f0d7-595a-b549-5b8b757eba5d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d35c11bd-f0d7-595a-b549-5b8b757eba5d" :step/id #uuid "d35c11bd-f0d7-595a-b549-5b8b757eba5d" :step/content 17592186365927 :step/next "step-57c40029-7a9d-590e-8482-18a486295420" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-57c40029-7a9d-590e-8482-18a486295420" :step/id #uuid "57c40029-7a9d-590e-8482-18a486295420" :step/content 17592186365933 :step/next "step-62326fde-cea3-5a99-a3e3-e672607ec1fa" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-62326fde-cea3-5a99-a3e3-e672607ec1fa" :step/id #uuid "62326fde-cea3-5a99-a3e3-e672607ec1fa" :step/content 17592186365936 :step/next "step-d7ac7bce-1bb6-5197-aa1a-34fb4ea4e07e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d7ac7bce-1bb6-5197-aa1a-34fb4ea4e07e" :step/id #uuid "d7ac7bce-1bb6-5197-aa1a-34fb4ea4e07e" :step/content "question-02669dc0-8a6e-5ef4-aa36-77e644af949e" :step/next "step-5fca549c-1acc-5d66-bb4d-25ca00cb6c5e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5fca549c-1acc-5d66-bb4d-25ca00cb6c5e" :step/id #uuid "5fca549c-1acc-5d66-bb4d-25ca00cb6c5e" :step/content 17592186365960 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186341494 :answer-field/choices 17592186339492] [:db/retract 17592186341494 :answer-field/choices 17592186340133] [:db/retract 17592186341494 :answer-field/choices 17592186340325] [:db/retract 17592186341494 :answer-field/choices 17592186339630] [:db/retract 17592186284019 :answer-field/choices 17592186283728] [:db/retract 17592186284019 :answer-field/choices 17592186282946] [:db/retract 17592186284019 :answer-field/choices 17592186283889] [:db/retract 17592186284019 :answer-field/choices 17592186283838] [:db/retract 17592186323343 :answer-field/choices 17592186322290] [:db/retract 17592186323343 :answer-field/choices 17592186321931] [:db/retract 17592186323343 :answer-field/choices 17592186321643] [:db/retract 17592186323343 :answer-field/choices 17592186321518] [:db/retract 17592186124882 :question/answer-fields 17592186121892] [:db/retract 17592186124881 :question/answer-fields 17592186121886] {:db/id 17592186124650 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-10b62984-455d-5702-af01-c69ee89bcccb" "question-a868cb2e-b1af-59f1-9123-572788886f02"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-10b62984-455d-5702-af01-c69ee89bcccb" :question/id #uuid "10b62984-455d-5702-af01-c69ee89bcccb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164281" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{9}=-1,$ find its $z$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{9} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{9} & =-1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-4$ and $z=2.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-41b2046a-1076-5804-ad5b-bbdc2b3b7878"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-41b2046a-1076-5804-ad5b-bbdc2b3b7878" :answer-field/id #uuid "41b2046a-1076-5804-ad5b-bbdc2b3b7878" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ef7480b-38c1-599a-b433-084149902947" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d8befc7-a689-5d26-9fc6-529209bfcbe1" "answer-bbf884f2-f6b1-51b5-8d26-4535c1ab921c" "answer-7ef7480b-38c1-599a-b433-084149902947" "answer-24f05d09-5866-526f-89e0-015abc3a350f" "answer-5d9e09ac-9afa-5c58-a5b6-e260d9ae3735"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d8befc7-a689-5d26-9fc6-529209bfcbe1" :answer/id #uuid "9d8befc7-a689-5d26-9fc6-529209bfcbe1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,-1)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbf884f2-f6b1-51b5-8d26-4535c1ab921c" :answer/id #uuid "bbf884f2-f6b1-51b5-8d26-4535c1ab921c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,-3)$ and $(0,0,3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ef7480b-38c1-599a-b433-084149902947" :answer/id #uuid "7ef7480b-38c1-599a-b433-084149902947" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24f05d09-5866-526f-89e0-015abc3a350f" :answer/id #uuid "24f05d09-5866-526f-89e0-015abc3a350f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,2)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9e09ac-9afa-5c58-a5b6-e260d9ae3735" :answer/id #uuid "5d9e09ac-9afa-5c58-a5b6-e260d9ae3735" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The hyperboloid does not intersect the $z$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a868cb2e-b1af-59f1-9123-572788886f02" :question/id #uuid "a868cb2e-b1af-59f1-9123-572788886f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-36475" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-3)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its $x$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}+1-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=2$ and $8.$\n\nTherefore,the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(2,0,0)$ and $(8,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e7a3d3c-5105-54a3-9154-3edb17af611a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e7a3d3c-5105-54a3-9154-3edb17af611a" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7a3d3c-5105-54a3-9154-3edb17af611a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a4e4424e-c714-595f-b617-bad4bcd7c52a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c96da8f2-38eb-5b21-ac5f-641d8aad25c4" "answer-58d2eb9c-609f-574a-a49a-f23703c4758e" "answer-a4e4424e-c714-595f-b617-bad4bcd7c52a" "answer-3503321b-5862-586b-8df4-8f5359f56cc1" "answer-eb172f3f-f1b6-5859-a6a9-6d63d2826e25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c96da8f2-38eb-5b21-ac5f-641d8aad25c4" :answer/id #uuid "c96da8f2-38eb-5b21-ac5f-641d8aad25c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-3,0,0)$ and $(3,0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58d2eb9c-609f-574a-a49a-f23703c4758e" :answer/id #uuid "58d2eb9c-609f-574a-a49a-f23703c4758e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-5\\sqrt{3},0,0)$ and $(5\\sqrt{3},0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4e4424e-c714-595f-b617-bad4bcd7c52a" :answer/id #uuid "a4e4424e-c714-595f-b617-bad4bcd7c52a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ and $(8,0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3503321b-5862-586b-8df4-8f5359f56cc1" :answer/id #uuid "3503321b-5862-586b-8df4-8f5359f56cc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb172f3f-f1b6-5859-a6a9-6d63d2826e25" :answer/id #uuid "eb172f3f-f1b6-5859-a6a9-6d63d2826e25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(5-\\sqrt{3},0,0)$ and $(5+\\sqrt{3},0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186342131 :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1 & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1) \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186341494 :answer-field/correct "answer-27a941c5-178a-59ae-85cf-65febbfce639" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27a941c5-178a-59ae-85cf-65febbfce639" "answer-b85f79a1-9c55-50f6-8994-c949dca618ab" "answer-7d19b694-4700-571e-bc5e-8170015e653a" "answer-278677bd-636d-5e6b-baa4-61d15bbe918b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27a941c5-178a-59ae-85cf-65febbfce639" :answer/id #uuid "27a941c5-178a-59ae-85cf-65febbfce639" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b85f79a1-9c55-50f6-8994-c949dca618ab" :answer/id #uuid "b85f79a1-9c55-50f6-8994-c949dca618ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d19b694-4700-571e-bc5e-8170015e653a" :answer/id #uuid "7d19b694-4700-571e-bc5e-8170015e653a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{7}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-278677bd-636d-5e6b-baa4-61d15bbe918b" :answer/id #uuid "278677bd-636d-5e6b-baa4-61d15bbe918b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284019 :answer-field/choices ["answer-c147bab5-dcbd-567a-bc0c-62bd9e31c5c2" "answer-9ab0c32b-18e1-5b35-a03b-9b03adc7d788" "answer-c6eae583-dc2b-5ab2-972a-742524065419" "answer-fce30ea6-bd76-50a8-a2fc-a7c8a2ca924e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c147bab5-dcbd-567a-bc0c-62bd9e31c5c2" :answer/id #uuid "c147bab5-dcbd-567a-bc0c-62bd9e31c5c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}<1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ab0c32b-18e1-5b35-a03b-9b03adc7d788" :answer/id #uuid "9ab0c32b-18e1-5b35-a03b-9b03adc7d788" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}>1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6eae583-dc2b-5ab2-972a-742524065419" :answer/id #uuid "c6eae583-dc2b-5ab2-972a-742524065419" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fce30ea6-bd76-50a8-a2fc-a7c8a2ca924e" :answer/id #uuid "fce30ea6-bd76-50a8-a2fc-a7c8a2ca924e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124988 :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =9((x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186323343 :answer-field/correct "answer-70f17460-a0d4-5287-abb4-a7a14abebf28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70f17460-a0d4-5287-abb4-a7a14abebf28" "answer-ea037c7d-5e1c-5139-8dac-7afde0a986e7" "answer-8ff9fece-b264-5543-a13e-24bcc8f1b95b" "answer-7f3f2b2f-aee1-5f7a-bf0b-2d4d8ee14ecf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70f17460-a0d4-5287-abb4-a7a14abebf28" :answer/id #uuid "70f17460-a0d4-5287-abb4-a7a14abebf28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea037c7d-5e1c-5139-8dac-7afde0a986e7" :answer/id #uuid "ea037c7d-5e1c-5139-8dac-7afde0a986e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ff9fece-b264-5543-a13e-24bcc8f1b95b" :answer/id #uuid "8ff9fece-b264-5543-a13e-24bcc8f1b95b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f3f2b2f-aee1-5f7a-bf0b-2d4d8ee14ecf" :answer/id #uuid "7f3f2b2f-aee1-5f7a-bf0b-2d4d8ee14ecf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125773 :tutorial/content "A **hyperboloid of one sheet** centered at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is a quadric surface whose equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of one sheet\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1\n$$\n\nis shown below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/0d/0d97e5ac26685b49bcdb3155866bd34ba83bd758f02035b500d3f594fc5716c8.png)\n\n   \n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the coordinate axes are as follows:\n\n- the $x$-intercepts are $(\\pm1,0,0)$\n\n- the $y$-intercepts are $(0,\\pm1,0)$\n\n- there are no $z$-intercepts" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125775 :tutorial/content "A **hyperboloid of two sheets** centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(z-z_0)^2}{c^2} =-1.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This equation is similar to the general equation of a hyperboloid of one sheet. The only difference is the right-hand side is now $-1$ instead of $+1.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperboloid of two sheets \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=-1\n$$\n\nis represented below. It has its center at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperboloid.\n\n![](images/96/96ec4196c3e299d2e57cdb8a3a2a06094452bf05e2f66e43fc60c059077b305f.png)\n\nThe intercepts of this hyperboloid with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,\\pm1).$ The surface does not intersect the $x$- or $y$-axes.\n\nCalculating the intercepts of a hyperboloid with the coordinate axes is done in the same way as we've seen before with spheres and ellipsoids. Let's see some examples.\n\nThere is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids of one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125879 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the graphs a typical one-sheet and two-sheet hyperboloids centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/45/45359d1c73d9cdc721068dbee0e27bedb8cde622bae0615c16862e4157b2baf3.png)\n\n   \n\nNotice that a hyperboloid of two sheets is defined for any $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ In fact, for any point $(x,y)$ in the $xy$-plane, there are exactly two points on the hyperboloid. \n\nOn the other hand, the graph of a hyperboloid of one sheet does not contain points over the central elliptical region (close to the axis of symmetry).\n\nFor example, given the hyperboloid of one sheet $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = 1,$ let's find the domain of $z = z(x,y).$\n\nFrom the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the *exterior* and boundary of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ for the hyperboloid $x^2+y^2-z^2 = 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\left \\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\geq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125877 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperboloid of one sheet are either ellipses or hyperbolas.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperboloid of one sheet given by\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2-z^2=1.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle centered at the origin of radius $1$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/b3/b369958a609ba3861be5d32fbf3195b6feb241228f81e89bb116b29f80e38318.png)\n\n   \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $xz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/4c/4c77b8758877ac88a73dece8c52f3b9cb650c4176edad5db020b138d70e0393b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a hyperbola in the $yz$-plane centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/0d/0ddcfc2e78166fa801a46deccd30c4cc2ddc12e81ae2e81508b5e13a374c208f.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124695 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105697 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0e7a888c-cec5-5044-9f5d-6a42f018a041" "question-0199ac32-0250-5816-84be-6c71995ddb1d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0e7a888c-cec5-5044-9f5d-6a42f018a041" :question/id #uuid "0e7a888c-cec5-5044-9f5d-6a42f018a041" :question/math-academy-id "q-134549" :question/problem "Determine the measure of angle $\\alpha .$\n\n$\\alpha =$  \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, angle $\\alpha $ is an interior angle of a regular octagon.\n\nA regular octagon is an $8$-sided polygon where all the interior angles have the same measure.\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles for the $n$-sided polygon equals\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\times (n-2).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a regular octagon is\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\times (8-2)=1\\,{080}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the measure of each interior angle is \n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{1\\,{080}^{∘}}{8}={135}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c242367-088a-5aaa-85d0-7576ac6b6d63"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c242367-088a-5aaa-85d0-7576ac6b6d63" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c242367-088a-5aaa-85d0-7576ac6b6d63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-789de0a7-45d5-544c-a969-e875ef116dc4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-789de0a7-45d5-544c-a969-e875ef116dc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-789de0a7-45d5-544c-a969-e875ef116dc4" :answer/id #uuid "789de0a7-45d5-544c-a969-e875ef116dc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110791 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-34ed8780-12db-5c6f-9281-2e2a475215ec"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-34ed8780-12db-5c6f-9281-2e2a475215ec" :question/id #uuid "34ed8780-12db-5c6f-9281-2e2a475215ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-134595" :question/problem "Polygon $A$ is congruent to polygon{{field-1}} (enter the polygon's label)." :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n* they have the same number of sides,\n\n* all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n* all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare polygons $B$ and $D$ to $A.$\n\n* The polygon $A$ is an octagon (it has $8$ sides). So, the polygons $B$ and $D$ can't be congruent to the polygon $A$ because they do not have $8$ sides.\n\n* On the other hand, $A$ and $C$ have the same shape: they have congruent corresponding sides, and since they are regular polygons, congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $A≅C.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d0833e5-39f9-58f0-b668-4023e8567dac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d0833e5-39f9-58f0-b668-4023e8567dac" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0833e5-39f9-58f0-b668-4023e8567dac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8d9e93a6-f563-5f14-80b7-9d9784360bfc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d9e93a6-f563-5f14-80b7-9d9784360bfc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d9e93a6-f563-5f14-80b7-9d9784360bfc" :answer/id #uuid "8d9e93a6-f563-5f14-80b7-9d9784360bfc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105807 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-160e2a67-96cf-5111-8246-74329f1cd311" "question-922cc406-bf6e-549f-8f16-dbc5eef7f87b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-160e2a67-96cf-5111-8246-74329f1cd311" :question/id #uuid "160e2a67-96cf-5111-8246-74329f1cd311" :question/math-academy-id "q-134550" :question/problem "Given that all four squares shown in the diagram are congruent, find the perimeter $p$ of square $B.$\n\n$p=$  \n{{field-1}}$cm$" :question/worked-solution "Since all the squares in the graph are congruent, their sides are of equal length. \n\nFrom the graph, the measure of one of the sides is $5\\,cm$, so all four sides of square $B$ have length $5\\,cm.$\n\nThe perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\np=4\\cdot 5=20\\,cm.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f34133c-427e-53d9-8698-dcabc78b675d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f34133c-427e-53d9-8698-dcabc78b675d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f34133c-427e-53d9-8698-dcabc78b675d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-27c32ff3-e119-59e1-9eed-671d1486d368" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27c32ff3-e119-59e1-9eed-671d1486d368"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27c32ff3-e119-59e1-9eed-671d1486d368" :answer/id #uuid "27c32ff3-e119-59e1-9eed-671d1486d368" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110783 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e9331587-ba62-51b2-9497-b1e12f18138c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9331587-ba62-51b2-9497-b1e12f18138c" :question/id #uuid "e9331587-ba62-51b2-9497-b1e12f18138c" :question/math-academy-id "q-134596" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ given that $\\beta =(2x+3)^{∘}.$\n\n$x=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\beta $ is an angle obtained after bisecting one of the interior angles of a square by a diagonal. Therefore, we must have \n\n$$\n\\beta =\\frac{{90}^{∘}}{2}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\beta  & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ (2x+3)^{∘} & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+3 & =45 \\\\ 2x & =42 \\\\ x & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-759b510a-b989-59da-81ab-d1d02eec0dfc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-759b510a-b989-59da-81ab-d1d02eec0dfc" :answer-field/id #uuid "759b510a-b989-59da-81ab-d1d02eec0dfc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-15afaf43-187f-5d31-999f-e1b1c9a4e9cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15afaf43-187f-5d31-999f-e1b1c9a4e9cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15afaf43-187f-5d31-999f-e1b1c9a4e9cc" :answer/id #uuid "15afaf43-187f-5d31-999f-e1b1c9a4e9cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0199ac32-0250-5816-84be-6c71995ddb1d" :question/id #uuid "0199ac32-0250-5816-84be-6c71995ddb1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-134600" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\gamma $, the angle shown in trapezoid $D.$\n\n$\\gamma =$  \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's label the vertices of trapezoid $D$ as follows:\n\n![](images/14/14814c5373405af8211f12b18f591c7a9c34645bfc8396b8304d6fcc73f5a539.png)\n\n \n\nIn step 1, we found that measures of the interior angles of a regular octagon equal\n\n$$\nm∠O=m∠N={135}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nThe sum of the measures of the interior angles for the trapezoid equals ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\gamma $ by summing the measures of the interior angles of the trapezoid $D.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M+m∠N+m∠O+m∠P & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ \\gamma +{135}^{∘}+{135}^{∘}+\\gamma  & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {270}^{∘}+2\\gamma  & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\gamma  & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ \\gamma  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-395ed277-0ca9-5298-b00a-892a1e31f04c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-395ed277-0ca9-5298-b00a-892a1e31f04c" :answer-field/id #uuid "395ed277-0ca9-5298-b00a-892a1e31f04c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a560515d-f0bc-56e0-9c3a-94762fd4cc0c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a560515d-f0bc-56e0-9c3a-94762fd4cc0c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a560515d-f0bc-56e0-9c3a-94762fd4cc0c" :answer/id #uuid "a560515d-f0bc-56e0-9c3a-94762fd4cc0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054656 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bf6b20ed-8b8a-511e-9502-26f8a79629b1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bf6b20ed-8b8a-511e-9502-26f8a79629b1" :question/id #uuid "bf6b20ed-8b8a-511e-9502-26f8a79629b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-134601" :question/problem "Given that $\\delta =(3y-15)^{∘},$ find the value of $y.$\n\n$y=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the two bases of the trapezoid $D$ are parallel, angles $\\delta $ and $\\gamma $ in the graph are alternate interior angles and consequently, they have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta  & =\\gamma ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta  & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ (3y-15)^{∘} & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ 3y-15 & =45 \\\\ 3y & =60 \\\\ y & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0cbcadfd-384e-5510-9480-433f1a05aef1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0cbcadfd-384e-5510-9480-433f1a05aef1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbcadfd-384e-5510-9480-433f1a05aef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5109a18c-60b0-56ed-8fb4-7e25ce741d69" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5109a18c-60b0-56ed-8fb4-7e25ce741d69"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5109a18c-60b0-56ed-8fb4-7e25ce741d69" :answer/id #uuid "5109a18c-60b0-56ed-8fb4-7e25ce741d69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105698 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e422e657-fc67-5f57-8e3f-8fd6f2f37149"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e422e657-fc67-5f57-8e3f-8fd6f2f37149" :question/id #uuid "e422e657-fc67-5f57-8e3f-8fd6f2f37149" :question/math-academy-id "q-134616" :question/problem "Find the measure of the exterior angle $\\omega .$\n\n$\\omega =$  \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\omega $ is the sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a regular octagon and a square.\n \n\n![](images/25/2586359b460d4e4ce0324b21caa9328f1d319ebdefa5f7118aaa4719973cb79d.png)\n\n \n\nFor any polygon, the exterior angles sum to ${360}^{∘}.$ In a regular octagon, there are $8$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{8}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, in a square, there are $4$ equivalent exterior angles, so each has a measure of \n\n$$\n\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{4}={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, since the shaded angle is the sum of the measures of exterior angles of a regular octagon and a square, it must have a measure of\n\n$$\n{45}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}={135}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e665187-2a28-5f13-bb23-18072eab0afd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e665187-2a28-5f13-bb23-18072eab0afd" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e665187-2a28-5f13-bb23-18072eab0afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1df22157-7c87-5d6c-8bb7-14b16e5f9f59" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1df22157-7c87-5d6c-8bb7-14b16e5f9f59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1df22157-7c87-5d6c-8bb7-14b16e5f9f59" :answer/id #uuid "1df22157-7c87-5d6c-8bb7-14b16e5f9f59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-922cc406-bf6e-549f-8f16-dbc5eef7f87b" :question/id #uuid "922cc406-bf6e-549f-8f16-dbc5eef7f87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-134617" :question/problem "Find the perimeter of the entire figure.\n\n$p=$  \n{{field-1}}$cm$" :question/worked-solution "The perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides.\n\nSince there are $24$ sides in total, and sides of the given figure are equal with length $5\\,cm$, we have \n\n$$\np=24\\cdot 5=120\\,cm.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4eb4c37a-2397-501c-afb5-87d9ae5c11c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4eb4c37a-2397-501c-afb5-87d9ae5c11c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb4c37a-2397-501c-afb5-87d9ae5c11c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6229feb1-b95d-595e-a375-c675e4dcfbe5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6229feb1-b95d-595e-a375-c675e4dcfbe5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6229feb1-b95d-595e-a375-c675e4dcfbe5" :answer/id #uuid "6229feb1-b95d-595e-a375-c675e4dcfbe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-3b2744b7-1cb0-57fe-b93d-862607556a2a" :multistep/id #uuid "3b2744b7-1cb0-57fe-b93d-862607556a2a" :multistep/context "![](images/03/0353900626189bbb7f009b5b36909ca584520325c2eb515047f48700bffdc2a5.png)\n\n \n\nA part of a mosaic consisting of regular octagons and squares is shown above." :multistep/steps ["step-42229977-027b-5fb0-8642-9ec41c9f66e2" "step-629ce222-cd6e-5b09-a05d-4fab322d8235" "step-8590ac05-1261-5904-b49b-9f553a79867a" "step-37891410-298a-5507-a41a-daa799b65788" "step-57464aba-791c-59a0-8216-21e1028be36a" "step-aedb7621-fad9-55ec-8e8a-ac1a5083ed6f" "step-243095fd-f2f7-5a43-8444-5d732b6aaddb" "step-46a3ffb7-b975-549b-b574-519f79cb1523"] :multistep/first-step "step-42229977-027b-5fb0-8642-9ec41c9f66e2" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-42229977-027b-5fb0-8642-9ec41c9f66e2" :step/id #uuid "42229977-027b-5fb0-8642-9ec41c9f66e2" :step/content "question-0e7a888c-cec5-5044-9f5d-6a42f018a041" :step/next "step-629ce222-cd6e-5b09-a05d-4fab322d8235" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-629ce222-cd6e-5b09-a05d-4fab322d8235" :step/id #uuid "629ce222-cd6e-5b09-a05d-4fab322d8235" :step/content "question-34ed8780-12db-5c6f-9281-2e2a475215ec" :step/next "step-8590ac05-1261-5904-b49b-9f553a79867a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8590ac05-1261-5904-b49b-9f553a79867a" :step/id #uuid "8590ac05-1261-5904-b49b-9f553a79867a" :step/content "question-160e2a67-96cf-5111-8246-74329f1cd311" :step/next "step-37891410-298a-5507-a41a-daa799b65788" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-37891410-298a-5507-a41a-daa799b65788" :step/id #uuid "37891410-298a-5507-a41a-daa799b65788" :step/content "question-e9331587-ba62-51b2-9497-b1e12f18138c" :step/next "step-57464aba-791c-59a0-8216-21e1028be36a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-57464aba-791c-59a0-8216-21e1028be36a" :step/id #uuid "57464aba-791c-59a0-8216-21e1028be36a" :step/content "question-0199ac32-0250-5816-84be-6c71995ddb1d" :step/next "step-aedb7621-fad9-55ec-8e8a-ac1a5083ed6f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-aedb7621-fad9-55ec-8e8a-ac1a5083ed6f" :step/id #uuid "aedb7621-fad9-55ec-8e8a-ac1a5083ed6f" :step/content "question-bf6b20ed-8b8a-511e-9502-26f8a79629b1" :step/next "step-243095fd-f2f7-5a43-8444-5d732b6aaddb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-243095fd-f2f7-5a43-8444-5d732b6aaddb" :step/id #uuid "243095fd-f2f7-5a43-8444-5d732b6aaddb" :step/content "question-e422e657-fc67-5f57-8e3f-8fd6f2f37149" :step/next "step-46a3ffb7-b975-549b-b574-519f79cb1523" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-46a3ffb7-b975-549b-b574-519f79cb1523" :step/id #uuid "46a3ffb7-b975-549b-b574-519f79cb1523" :step/content "question-922cc406-bf6e-549f-8f16-dbc5eef7f87b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-fa5d8411-d4a1-5b32-ac8c-8e8a3fb8068d" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "fa5d8411-d4a1-5b32-ac8c-8e8a3fb8068d" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-3b2744b7-1cb0-57fe-b93d-862607556a2a" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186050514 17592186051041 17592186048921 17592186048360 17592186048673] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-613ee47d-ed16-5d5c-920b-9e3c6cc24f5a" :activity/id #uuid "613ee47d-ed16-5d5c-920b-9e3c6cc24f5a" :activity/title "Tiling the Plane With Regular Polygons" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-209e9668-9299-5ef2-8569-2207f557593d"] :activity/first-step "step-209e9668-9299-5ef2-8569-2207f557593d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-209e9668-9299-5ef2-8569-2207f557593d" :step/id #uuid "209e9668-9299-5ef2-8569-2207f557593d" :step/content "assigned-problem-fa5d8411-d4a1-5b32-ac8c-8e8a3fb8068d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198407 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c6e7ac3c-60bd-5c96-a837-e70ac483b403"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c6e7ac3c-60bd-5c96-a837-e70ac483b403" :question/id #uuid "c6e7ac3c-60bd-5c96-a837-e70ac483b403" :question/math-academy-id "q-162972" :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,-y\\le x\\le y\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y[{\\int }_{-y}^{y}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\cdot {[{x}^{3}]}_{x=-y}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\cdot 2{y}^{3}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b8a70bd-cbd3-5bb7-9a20-2989c5769039"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b8a70bd-cbd3-5bb7-9a20-2989c5769039" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8a70bd-cbd3-5bb7-9a20-2989c5769039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eae7226a-9ab7-55eb-9547-35faebb62155" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63becd05-2827-54e1-b25f-49475c7277d4" "answer-ccd1812f-96df-5ea4-86f6-fc9ebbe2a707" "answer-b7617195-3a4c-5cce-a014-90302c0f45d8" "answer-a8e556e3-1b58-565c-8179-f27e71c39ec1" "answer-eae7226a-9ab7-55eb-9547-35faebb62155"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63becd05-2827-54e1-b25f-49475c7277d4" :answer/id #uuid "63becd05-2827-54e1-b25f-49475c7277d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccd1812f-96df-5ea4-86f6-fc9ebbe2a707" :answer/id #uuid "ccd1812f-96df-5ea4-86f6-fc9ebbe2a707" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7617195-3a4c-5cce-a014-90302c0f45d8" :answer/id #uuid "b7617195-3a4c-5cce-a014-90302c0f45d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8e556e3-1b58-565c-8179-f27e71c39ec1" :answer/id #uuid "a8e556e3-1b58-565c-8179-f27e71c39ec1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eae7226a-9ab7-55eb-9547-35faebb62155" :answer/id #uuid "eae7226a-9ab7-55eb-9547-35faebb62155" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281560 :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{y}\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198406 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1263ed3f-1cc0-53b6-a6ed-69fefa90cd2b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281587 :question/problem "The lamina $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=5xy.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1263ed3f-1cc0-53b6-a6ed-69fefa90cd2b" :question/id #uuid "1263ed3f-1cc0-53b6-a6ed-69fefa90cd2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163164" :question/problem "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{15{y}^{2}}{x}$ is enclosed between the curve  $x=2-{y}^{2}$ and the line $x=1$ for $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6c78767d03e3429551498e651d982d99b6ceb72aa73422c5205bef296c9a1754.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{15{y}^{2}}{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{15{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}15{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2-{y}^{2}}15{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{y}^{2}[x]_{x=1}^{x=2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{y}^{2}(2-{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{y}^{2}-15{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[5{y}^{3}-3{y}^{5}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(5-3)-(-5+3) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b671f817-f30d-58d1-9ad7-bc1f02d0b0c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b671f817-f30d-58d1-9ad7-bc1f02d0b0c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "b671f817-f30d-58d1-9ad7-bc1f02d0b0c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9f938d30-ac1a-58c2-b1e4-72cbfb1471d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b78bae3-6dac-5231-bc72-0f224493d1ba" "answer-461dd4bf-3120-534f-903c-0f6b43aa0379" "answer-9ee1ba58-3c47-5256-98f6-5f3213ad1570" "answer-9f938d30-ac1a-58c2-b1e4-72cbfb1471d2" "answer-5f7de461-b87c-5895-81fe-a20fba2fc2fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b78bae3-6dac-5231-bc72-0f224493d1ba" :answer/id #uuid "2b78bae3-6dac-5231-bc72-0f224493d1ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-461dd4bf-3120-534f-903c-0f6b43aa0379" :answer/id #uuid "461dd4bf-3120-534f-903c-0f6b43aa0379" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ee1ba58-3c47-5256-98f6-5f3213ad1570" :answer/id #uuid "9ee1ba58-3c47-5256-98f6-5f3213ad1570" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f938d30-ac1a-58c2-b1e4-72cbfb1471d2" :answer/id #uuid "9f938d30-ac1a-58c2-b1e4-72cbfb1471d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f7de461-b87c-5895-81fe-a20fba2fc2fd" :answer/id #uuid "5f7de461-b87c-5895-81fe-a20fba2fc2fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198365 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-567a0e9f-c2af-5671-a94c-0c227edb1ded"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-567a0e9f-c2af-5671-a94c-0c227edb1ded" :question/id #uuid "567a0e9f-c2af-5671-a94c-0c227edb1ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-163505" :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{{y}^{3}},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$ Given that the area of the lamina is $\\frac{2}{5}$ square units, find the $y$-coordinate of its centroid." :question/worked-solution "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y[x]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{[\\frac{2{y}^{7/2}}{7}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77626c7e-6f8b-519e-9042-3f623f612a29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77626c7e-6f8b-519e-9042-3f623f612a29" :answer-field/id #uuid "77626c7e-6f8b-519e-9042-3f623f612a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e1b7da3-c426-532e-ac8e-720cc44224b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71ace97c-298f-5384-84e5-5cec05c12f1a" "answer-6511608d-c3ac-5e2b-8914-4eba27b60808" "answer-6a9602ff-82fc-59fa-9526-6670ef4a4c21" "answer-72c7b13e-659a-509a-a72a-ffc4f4cc555e" "answer-1e1b7da3-c426-532e-ac8e-720cc44224b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71ace97c-298f-5384-84e5-5cec05c12f1a" :answer/id #uuid "71ace97c-298f-5384-84e5-5cec05c12f1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6511608d-c3ac-5e2b-8914-4eba27b60808" :answer/id #uuid "6511608d-c3ac-5e2b-8914-4eba27b60808" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a9602ff-82fc-59fa-9526-6670ef4a4c21" :answer/id #uuid "6a9602ff-82fc-59fa-9526-6670ef4a4c21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72c7b13e-659a-509a-a72a-ffc4f4cc555e" :answer/id #uuid "72c7b13e-659a-509a-a72a-ffc4f4cc555e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e1b7da3-c426-532e-ac8e-720cc44224b0" :answer/id #uuid "1e1b7da3-c426-532e-ac8e-720cc44224b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-66c5f538-bf73-5738-ac9e-056fec289e7e" :activity/id #uuid "66c5f538-bf73-5738-ac9e-056fec289e7e" :activity/title "Moments and Centers of Mass of Plane Laminas" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049110 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-64412876-71ee-5320-bcb3-58c33e18f0b7" "step-e84e8b2b-1d47-5a7c-a608-fa747bd1a9cf" "step-04149e10-de16-5e52-9d4c-e924df4e6c9a" "step-f8e51044-c774-57f1-9ffb-21331bca50b2" "step-edd8ba29-7f85-5fb5-96c7-3b323408b5ac"] :activity/first-step "step-64412876-71ee-5320-bcb3-58c33e18f0b7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-64412876-71ee-5320-bcb3-58c33e18f0b7" :step/id #uuid "64412876-71ee-5320-bcb3-58c33e18f0b7" :step/content "question-c6e7ac3c-60bd-5c96-a837-e70ac483b403" :step/next "step-e84e8b2b-1d47-5a7c-a608-fa747bd1a9cf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e84e8b2b-1d47-5a7c-a608-fa747bd1a9cf" :step/id #uuid "e84e8b2b-1d47-5a7c-a608-fa747bd1a9cf" :step/content 17592186281560 :step/next "step-04149e10-de16-5e52-9d4c-e924df4e6c9a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-04149e10-de16-5e52-9d4c-e924df4e6c9a" :step/id #uuid "04149e10-de16-5e52-9d4c-e924df4e6c9a" :step/content 17592186281587 :step/next "step-f8e51044-c774-57f1-9ffb-21331bca50b2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f8e51044-c774-57f1-9ffb-21331bca50b2" :step/id #uuid "f8e51044-c774-57f1-9ffb-21331bca50b2" :step/content "question-1263ed3f-1cc0-53b6-a6ed-69fefa90cd2b" :step/next "step-edd8ba29-7f85-5fb5-96c7-3b323408b5ac" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-edd8ba29-7f85-5fb5-96c7-3b323408b5ac" :step/id #uuid "edd8ba29-7f85-5fb5-96c7-3b323408b5ac" :step/content "question-567a0e9f-c2af-5671-a94c-0c227edb1ded" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059631 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0589289f-bd62-59fb-8540-3927d969e746"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0589289f-bd62-59fb-8540-3927d969e746" :question/id #uuid "0589289f-bd62-59fb-8540-3927d969e746" :question/math-academy-id "q-1663" :question/problem "If the hypotenuse of a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle has a length of $2$, then what is the length of each leg?" :question/worked-solution "The hypotenuse in a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $2.$ So, if the length of a leg is $a,$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71e2b994-15e5-5aeb-82fd-d4bc2277603b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71e2b994-15e5-5aeb-82fd-d4bc2277603b" :answer-field/id #uuid "71e2b994-15e5-5aeb-82fd-d4bc2277603b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c3eab157-7b55-56d1-9b81-180236a85206" :answer-field/choices ["answer-665ff479-5f6c-53a5-8058-2fe44ac3785d" "answer-5f9feb6b-dcf3-574d-a90e-17e5696df9b7" "answer-bba14005-dce5-5914-a761-712d664bb63a" "answer-aa4ee9ac-349c-5bdf-9164-c6193cd4e551" "answer-c3eab157-7b55-56d1-9b81-180236a85206"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-665ff479-5f6c-53a5-8058-2fe44ac3785d" :answer/id #uuid "665ff479-5f6c-53a5-8058-2fe44ac3785d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f9feb6b-dcf3-574d-a90e-17e5696df9b7" :answer/id #uuid "5f9feb6b-dcf3-574d-a90e-17e5696df9b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bba14005-dce5-5914-a761-712d664bb63a" :answer/id #uuid "bba14005-dce5-5914-a761-712d664bb63a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa4ee9ac-349c-5bdf-9164-c6193cd4e551" :answer/id #uuid "aa4ee9ac-349c-5bdf-9164-c6193cd4e551" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3eab157-7b55-56d1-9b81-180236a85206" :answer/id #uuid "c3eab157-7b55-56d1-9b81-180236a85206" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059638 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ed931f9b-ec89-504b-8f3a-6fc6233992c9" "question-d933a602-cca3-50d1-9290-6754d9186974"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ed931f9b-ec89-504b-8f3a-6fc6233992c9" :question/id #uuid "ed931f9b-ec89-504b-8f3a-6fc6233992c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-82302" :question/problem "![](images/27/2788f2795065599063c67d9ae7e9e6ee17cae4aa21198450faf7c09ad10765e1.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $6+3\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg." :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=6+3\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 6+3\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 3\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 3 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-563ab28a-360a-5519-9504-d8c10d6a3bc9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-563ab28a-360a-5519-9504-d8c10d6a3bc9" :answer-field/id #uuid "563ab28a-360a-5519-9504-d8c10d6a3bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa1bd1ce-5d7c-5356-8a41-6149fd10be2a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa1bd1ce-5d7c-5356-8a41-6149fd10be2a" "answer-b21ebaaf-7316-5810-a179-40920135f9f4" "answer-e5291e91-5e75-563e-9047-4ac169fda707" "answer-32fcd00f-a39a-58b1-925b-6f97038dc7ff" "answer-7a5e9904-c441-5249-95eb-da32c2bf31f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa1bd1ce-5d7c-5356-8a41-6149fd10be2a" :answer/id #uuid "fa1bd1ce-5d7c-5356-8a41-6149fd10be2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b21ebaaf-7316-5810-a179-40920135f9f4" :answer/id #uuid "b21ebaaf-7316-5810-a179-40920135f9f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5291e91-5e75-563e-9047-4ac169fda707" :answer/id #uuid "e5291e91-5e75-563e-9047-4ac169fda707" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32fcd00f-a39a-58b1-925b-6f97038dc7ff" :answer/id #uuid "32fcd00f-a39a-58b1-925b-6f97038dc7ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a5e9904-c441-5249-95eb-da32c2bf31f9" :answer/id #uuid "7a5e9904-c441-5249-95eb-da32c2bf31f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d933a602-cca3-50d1-9290-6754d9186974" :question/id #uuid "d933a602-cca3-50d1-9290-6754d9186974" :question/math-academy-id "q-82303" :question/problem "![](images/70/70d961102240b0c48132baca8db0b77ed80ff8e0d3b7296bd5c727ab2f97dfd3.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $10+5\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg." :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=10+5\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 10+5\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 5\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 5 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3915ce74-cbdd-56d7-8c28-9b915fda8173"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3915ce74-cbdd-56d7-8c28-9b915fda8173" :answer-field/id #uuid "3915ce74-cbdd-56d7-8c28-9b915fda8173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5288f03e-bcc1-596c-b630-e9cce9ef441f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b789fe7c-a826-5709-8e86-6a1815f091c2" "answer-241e62f7-500f-5996-9c45-a5607a120603" "answer-5288f03e-bcc1-596c-b630-e9cce9ef441f" "answer-e77d63d4-3768-57eb-87f1-5ab52552457c" "answer-628e1648-d1f4-5cdd-a67a-e29de5ad49a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b789fe7c-a826-5709-8e86-6a1815f091c2" :answer/id #uuid "b789fe7c-a826-5709-8e86-6a1815f091c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-241e62f7-500f-5996-9c45-a5607a120603" :answer/id #uuid "241e62f7-500f-5996-9c45-a5607a120603" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5288f03e-bcc1-596c-b630-e9cce9ef441f" :answer/id #uuid "5288f03e-bcc1-596c-b630-e9cce9ef441f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e77d63d4-3768-57eb-87f1-5ab52552457c" :answer/id #uuid "e77d63d4-3768-57eb-87f1-5ab52552457c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-628e1648-d1f4-5cdd-a67a-e29de5ad49a3" :answer/id #uuid "628e1648-d1f4-5cdd-a67a-e29de5ad49a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d4e27f16-3ada-5048-ad9f-c15bdd8faa37" :activity/id #uuid "d4e27f16-3ada-5048-ad9f-c15bdd8faa37" :activity/title "Isosceles Right Triangles" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048121 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-e2059ad8-8e80-5bdc-ac88-748ce810f290" "step-8730d12c-cd05-5ba6-99b2-4dbdb27838ea" "step-a6f7ec19-e013-5d9c-bcfa-d6001e21386c"] :activity/first-step "step-e2059ad8-8e80-5bdc-ac88-748ce810f290" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-e2059ad8-8e80-5bdc-ac88-748ce810f290" :step/id #uuid "e2059ad8-8e80-5bdc-ac88-748ce810f290" :step/content "question-0589289f-bd62-59fb-8540-3927d969e746" :step/next "step-8730d12c-cd05-5ba6-99b2-4dbdb27838ea" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8730d12c-cd05-5ba6-99b2-4dbdb27838ea" :step/id #uuid "8730d12c-cd05-5ba6-99b2-4dbdb27838ea" :step/content "question-ed931f9b-ec89-504b-8f3a-6fc6233992c9" :step/next "step-a6f7ec19-e013-5d9c-bcfa-d6001e21386c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a6f7ec19-e013-5d9c-bcfa-d6001e21386c" :step/id #uuid "a6f7ec19-e013-5d9c-bcfa-d6001e21386c" :step/content "question-d933a602-cca3-50d1-9290-6754d9186974" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e5e67c0f-856d-5d0f-b071-fa5f8bea8978" "question-7fbc634f-eb6c-5cc5-8193-44a06f615641" "question-d512c5fc-8dfe-51a8-bc6a-9cb26a626d2d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124313 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f9f121db-5810-54db-9c64-4a2b5a9ca0c0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5e67c0f-856d-5d0f-b071-fa5f8bea8978" :question/id #uuid "e5e67c0f-856d-5d0f-b071-fa5f8bea8978" :question/math-academy-id "q-164266" :question/problem "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3} & =3 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=1,7.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb1a33df-5d89-5bd8-acbb-0f22b0025fba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb1a33df-5d89-5bd8-acbb-0f22b0025fba" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1a33df-5d89-5bd8-acbb-0f22b0025fba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fc33ddfa-52a3-5cab-9230-240dbda3509c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cefd866e-fde1-54f8-bb4d-fcb772aa52fe" "answer-fc33ddfa-52a3-5cab-9230-240dbda3509c" "answer-40e995cc-1d5e-5d2c-a0a5-7fe3481e4650" "answer-d323e773-8ce1-5a22-bbc6-686f6e69e29d" "answer-308e7a40-21a0-5e19-8794-5ef50519ae84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cefd866e-fde1-54f8-bb4d-fcb772aa52fe" :answer/id #uuid "cefd866e-fde1-54f8-bb4d-fcb772aa52fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,0,0)$ and $(3,0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc33ddfa-52a3-5cab-9230-240dbda3509c" :answer/id #uuid "fc33ddfa-52a3-5cab-9230-240dbda3509c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40e995cc-1d5e-5d2c-a0a5-7fe3481e4650" :answer/id #uuid "40e995cc-1d5e-5d2c-a0a5-7fe3481e4650" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3,0,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d323e773-8ce1-5a22-bbc6-686f6e69e29d" :answer/id #uuid "d323e773-8ce1-5a22-bbc6-686f6e69e29d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(4,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-308e7a40-21a0-5e19-8794-5ef50519ae84" :answer/id #uuid "308e7a40-21a0-5e19-8794-5ef50519ae84" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(4,0,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fbc634f-eb6c-5cc5-8193-44a06f615641" :question/id #uuid "7fbc634f-eb6c-5cc5-8193-44a06f615641" :question/math-academy-id "q-164271" :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{2}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nz-3=\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-(-5))^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(2,-5,3).$\n Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89a28f00-1479-5316-83de-18eb1849ee16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89a28f00-1479-5316-83de-18eb1849ee16" :answer-field/id #uuid "89a28f00-1479-5316-83de-18eb1849ee16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-feef708c-0cde-5f9f-b38b-2fd9f6aeaa6d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e9806de-497f-584c-b86a-1e0bf84a4f89" "answer-e9ea8fd2-2ede-5c18-a3ed-6bc1cae2ef28" "answer-feef708c-0cde-5f9f-b38b-2fd9f6aeaa6d" "answer-8a88ecbb-9302-5042-b7a3-4c101edb8cb5" "answer-d0196b99-3a49-523b-bf63-aceb60f66036"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e9806de-497f-584c-b86a-1e0bf84a4f89" :answer/id #uuid "6e9806de-497f-584c-b86a-1e0bf84a4f89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ y=5\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9ea8fd2-2ede-5c18-a3ed-6bc1cae2ef28" :answer/id #uuid "e9ea8fd2-2ede-5c18-a3ed-6bc1cae2ef28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-feef708c-0cde-5f9f-b38b-2fd9f6aeaa6d" :answer/id #uuid "feef708c-0cde-5f9f-b38b-2fd9f6aeaa6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=-5\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a88ecbb-9302-5042-b7a3-4c101edb8cb5" :answer/id #uuid "8a88ecbb-9302-5042-b7a3-4c101edb8cb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0196b99-3a49-523b-bf63-aceb60f66036" :answer/id #uuid "d0196b99-3a49-523b-bf63-aceb60f66036" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=-5\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d512c5fc-8dfe-51a8-bc6a-9cb26a626d2d" :question/id #uuid "d512c5fc-8dfe-51a8-bc6a-9cb26a626d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72190" :question/problem "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=2(x+2)^{2}+5(y-3)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+4 & =2(x+2)^{2}+5(y-3)^{2} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{1/5} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{1/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(-2,3,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bcf0fc0c-4862-5e2c-8784-7233b51e1a00"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bcf0fc0c-4862-5e2c-8784-7233b51e1a00" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf0fc0c-4862-5e2c-8784-7233b51e1a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-45bf9d16-46f6-5ad4-945c-23e0fa193905" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45bf9d16-46f6-5ad4-945c-23e0fa193905" "answer-4920bf3c-5b17-591d-afbe-69b728b7a938" "answer-93160ae8-e21c-5ffe-8eec-80c436e786bb" "answer-bf85d86c-da6e-5d00-8220-fdd69b13e47a" "answer-7bf430df-7fb6-5716-a81c-1c5fa3681936"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45bf9d16-46f6-5ad4-945c-23e0fa193905" :answer/id #uuid "45bf9d16-46f6-5ad4-945c-23e0fa193905" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,3,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4920bf3c-5b17-591d-afbe-69b728b7a938" :answer/id #uuid "4920bf3c-5b17-591d-afbe-69b728b7a938" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93160ae8-e21c-5ffe-8eec-80c436e786bb" :answer/id #uuid "93160ae8-e21c-5ffe-8eec-80c436e786bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-3,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf85d86c-da6e-5d00-8220-fdd69b13e47a" :answer/id #uuid "bf85d86c-da6e-5d00-8220-fdd69b13e47a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-3,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bf430df-7fb6-5716-a81c-1c5fa3681936" :answer/id #uuid "7bf430df-7fb6-5716-a81c-1c5fa3681936" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-2,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124897 :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=100(z-2)$ and the plane \n$z=7$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=7$, we substitute $z=7$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=100(z-2) \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=100(7-2) \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+25(y+1)^{2}=500 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{125}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{20} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{5})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $5\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121982 :answer-field/correct 17592186121983 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9f121db-5810-54db-9c64-4a2b5a9ca0c0" :question/id #uuid "f9f121db-5810-54db-9c64-4a2b5a9ca0c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-164280" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-1=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-1 & =0-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-1 & =-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-2(z-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4791e86d-9b5c-5667-8c55-2ae5f7cc7bea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4791e86d-9b5c-5667-8c55-2ae5f7cc7bea" :answer-field/id #uuid "4791e86d-9b5c-5667-8c55-2ae5f7cc7bea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ac05287-9a63-56f4-8c38-1478d40a3c4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d2b9c95-99a6-5000-942b-75a84d95cf3f" "answer-0ac05287-9a63-56f4-8c38-1478d40a3c4b" "answer-a744e195-87e4-5b74-a1ee-ac3319ce566d" "answer-4fda1058-1427-5a2b-80fc-c2f1e5cb1db2" "answer-3eef7421-320f-5e5c-b4e9-248012094a82"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d2b9c95-99a6-5000-942b-75a84d95cf3f" :answer/id #uuid "5d2b9c95-99a6-5000-942b-75a84d95cf3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A hyperbola: $2(y+1)^{2}-{z}^{2}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ac05287-9a63-56f4-8c38-1478d40a3c4b" :answer/id #uuid "0ac05287-9a63-56f4-8c38-1478d40a3c4b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A parabola: $(y+1)^{2}=-2(z-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a744e195-87e4-5b74-a1ee-ac3319ce566d" :answer/id #uuid "a744e195-87e4-5b74-a1ee-ac3319ce566d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A parabola: ${y}^{2}=2(z-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fda1058-1427-5a2b-80fc-c2f1e5cb1db2" :answer/id #uuid "4fda1058-1427-5a2b-80fc-c2f1e5cb1db2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 2(z-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eef7421-320f-5e5c-b4e9-248012094a82" :answer/id #uuid "3eef7421-320f-5e5c-b4e9-248012094a82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $z=4(y\\pm 1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125774 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadric surface with the equation\n\n$$\nz = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\n \n\nThis surface is an example of an **elliptic paraboloid.**\n\n The **vertex** of this elliptic paraboloid is located at the origin, and its **axis of symmetry** is the $z$-axis. Note that the axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis whose vertex is at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nThese surfaces are called *elliptic* *paraboloids* because their traces in the $xy$-plane are *ellipses*, while their traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125776 :tutorial/content "Now consider the graph of the quadric surface\n\n$$\nz = x^2 -y^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f02c207742c737d00cdeabbd2cbba93a88a42521c41a2cc1e9f19d27ff55f20.png)\n\n \n\nThis surface is an example of a **hyperbolic paraboloid** whose **saddle point** is at the origin.\n\nMore generally, a hyperbolic paraboloid whose saddle point is at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ has the general equation\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2}- \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ constants. \n\nThis surface is called *hyperbolic* *paraboloid* because its traces in the $xy$-plane are *hyperbolas*, while its traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly.\n\nAnother property is that this surface mirrors itself across the planes $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Since these planes meet along the $z$-axis, that axis serves as the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperbolic paraboloid." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125805 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered elliptic paraboloids whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. \nLet's now consider the cases when an elliptic paraboloid is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows.\n\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis:\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/da/da37415ddd27764a719fecc5780ee91e6e8ad3f5ce877101b81d1a955c57eb6f.png)\n\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d1488d9f4767312795a84ce6441f64dd567223d1de4da66adbd6e39de9612a53.png)\n\nLikewise, for a hyperbolic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis. The two corresponding equations are\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}.\n$$\n\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis. The two corresponding equations are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nx = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125880 :tutorial/content "The traces of an elliptic paraboloid are either ellipses, parabolas, a single point, or the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic paraboloid given by\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/02/021dd988863021f564a0e85fb3db93030740059f2a50ade6504b3e12a5665372.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ {x}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/9f/9f6cd0703b5d7b2458cbfd688d91f08a79752fa1ccd3616e5975f5c017aa7b42.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ {y}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/89/89be283ed121d3e30b1913e4412b8d3556ed611ef2a9a9bfd686d0594409f5c9.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=-k$ for $k > 0$ is the empty set. This is because $z\\geq 0$ everywhere on the elliptic paraboloid.\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k > 0$ is an ellipse:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = k\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f15e925431b0d2ccfd4afa703cad1bcb37b65a75e96fbeabcf4823703bf184e5.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125881 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperbolic paraboloid are either hyperbolas, parabolas, or intersecting lines.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperbolic paraboloid given by\n\n$$\nz=x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two intersecting lines in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9b/9b3fbb9d8945c5d9208a59d404e489e4a339082c602c18154010e3e53083b220.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & ={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/e8/e8e2b6306052e781fd149e4eebb04da79eddb44b5261b05b129f208ad523b70b.png)\n\n \n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & =-{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/70/7010e4c26af60c6c27f66c93b805a2bac1e191c1646b5aac3a77f5dd3dadc64f.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular case, the **saddle point** is at the origin. It is a minimum point for the trace in the $xz$-plane but a maximum point for the trace in the $yz$-plane.\n\nFinally, note that the trace of our surface with the plane $z=k\\neq 0$ is a hyperbola:\n\n$$\nx^2-y^2 = k\n$$\n\n![](images/b9/b9c1baab2f9d85c2c415cae91908d5c908aa0b1cef1eb8b5901f7f3b78bf66b2.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124696 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d0bffce7-58da-5042-ae7f-fb5de551e1a7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203852 :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\nII. $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\nIII. $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6feec1bb-9d5b-5e0f-99d7-930f0577f85b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204091 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0f18d6f2-f26d-5ef1-84dd-3ecfcc80ad41" "question-e39fec5d-501d-5ef0-88cc-5eeb84d919b1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d0bffce7-58da-5042-ae7f-fb5de551e1a7" :question/id #uuid "d0bffce7-58da-5042-ae7f-fb5de551e1a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-288069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $2x-3=2x+4$\n\nII. $-5x+7=-2x-2$\n\nIII. $4x+1=4x+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =2x+4 \\\\ 2x-3-2x & =2x+4-2x \\\\ -3 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x-3=2x+4$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+7 & =-2x-2 \\\\ -5x+7+2x-7 & =-2x-2+2x-7 \\\\ -3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-5x+7=-2x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4dd41eb7-fc07-5b0d-8a34-e949bc172ef0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dd41eb7-fc07-5b0d-8a34-e949bc172ef0" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dd41eb7-fc07-5b0d-8a34-e949bc172ef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0c78cb3d-2cc5-5e2c-9e55-a80e139339bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-750f8f74-e77a-5d2e-8f3d-ca74279509fa" "answer-306bd0fa-00bf-5a91-966b-275a7a3521a4" "answer-0c78cb3d-2cc5-5e2c-9e55-a80e139339bf" "answer-b5b51887-d353-59f8-948b-51c3d03accf9" "answer-b8fb200b-60c0-5f70-ae27-f8dd1334ced2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-750f8f74-e77a-5d2e-8f3d-ca74279509fa" :answer/id #uuid "750f8f74-e77a-5d2e-8f3d-ca74279509fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-306bd0fa-00bf-5a91-966b-275a7a3521a4" :answer/id #uuid "306bd0fa-00bf-5a91-966b-275a7a3521a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c78cb3d-2cc5-5e2c-9e55-a80e139339bf" :answer/id #uuid "0c78cb3d-2cc5-5e2c-9e55-a80e139339bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5b51887-d353-59f8-948b-51c3d03accf9" :answer/id #uuid "b5b51887-d353-59f8-948b-51c3d03accf9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8fb200b-60c0-5f70-ae27-f8dd1334ced2" :answer/id #uuid "b8fb200b-60c0-5f70-ae27-f8dd1334ced2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204596 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2px=3\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203490 :answer-field/correct 17592186203491 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-6feec1bb-9d5b-5e0f-99d7-930f0577f85b" :question/id #uuid "6feec1bb-9d5b-5e0f-99d7-930f0577f85b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288107" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $t,$ given by\n\n$$\n5qt=-4\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $at=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=5q,\\,b=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5q=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\nq=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa2138e2-b337-52b3-8a3d-5fd1ad2f1eba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa2138e2-b337-52b3-8a3d-5fd1ad2f1eba" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2138e2-b337-52b3-8a3d-5fd1ad2f1eba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9bd678d9-2685-5d26-b56a-e4126c2c4ff5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9bd678d9-2685-5d26-b56a-e4126c2c4ff5" "answer-a039287f-5135-5191-970c-859892a6a41c" "answer-4ba4ea7d-61a8-5364-be2d-d234f8cf3638" "answer-27628a6f-5c23-5872-87a7-878f2e1da6b2" "answer-b5a20a9e-5f1a-5668-bd6f-c5fd978a900f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bd678d9-2685-5d26-b56a-e4126c2c4ff5" :answer/id #uuid "9bd678d9-2685-5d26-b56a-e4126c2c4ff5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a039287f-5135-5191-970c-859892a6a41c" :answer/id #uuid "a039287f-5135-5191-970c-859892a6a41c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ba4ea7d-61a8-5364-be2d-d234f8cf3638" :answer/id #uuid "4ba4ea7d-61a8-5364-be2d-d234f8cf3638" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27628a6f-5c23-5872-87a7-878f2e1da6b2" :answer/id #uuid "27628a6f-5c23-5872-87a7-878f2e1da6b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5a20a9e-5f1a-5668-bd6f-c5fd978a900f" :answer/id #uuid "b5a20a9e-5f1a-5668-bd6f-c5fd978a900f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f18d6f2-f26d-5ef1-84dd-3ecfcc80ad41" :question/id #uuid "0f18d6f2-f26d-5ef1-84dd-3ecfcc80ad41" :question/math-academy-id "q-288639" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(q-7)v+5=1\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7)v+5 & =1 \\\\ 2(q-7)v+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ {{2(q-7)}_{⏟}}_{a}v & ={{-4}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(q-7)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(q-7)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ q-7 & =0 \\\\ q & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6fdf80ca-384b-523b-a17d-5e97bc529c52"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6fdf80ca-384b-523b-a17d-5e97bc529c52" :answer-field/id #uuid "6fdf80ca-384b-523b-a17d-5e97bc529c52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3b304e2a-8f8e-5e89-a6a1-5c5b87fcc267" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b304e2a-8f8e-5e89-a6a1-5c5b87fcc267"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b304e2a-8f8e-5e89-a6a1-5c5b87fcc267" :answer/id #uuid "3b304e2a-8f8e-5e89-a6a1-5c5b87fcc267" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e39fec5d-501d-5ef0-88cc-5eeb84d919b1" :question/id #uuid "e39fec5d-501d-5ef0-88cc-5eeb84d919b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288637" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-7(k+3)z+8=1\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(k+3)z+8 & =1 \\\\ -7(k+3)z+8-8 & =1-8 \\\\ {{-7(k+3)}_{⏟}}_{a}z & ={{-7}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-7(k+3)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(k+3) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-7(k+3)}{-7} & =\\frac{0}{-7} \\\\ k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11a9a15b-5aa0-5680-a061-a7bfedf60bfd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11a9a15b-5aa0-5680-a061-a7bfedf60bfd" :answer-field/id #uuid "11a9a15b-5aa0-5680-a061-a7bfedf60bfd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f6b9eab8-ff3a-501f-9616-28e24c66aaf9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6b9eab8-ff3a-501f-9616-28e24c66aaf9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6b9eab8-ff3a-501f-9616-28e24c66aaf9" :answer/id #uuid "f6b9eab8-ff3a-501f-9616-28e24c66aaf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205207 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7qx = -5,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solutions, what is the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = b{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{-5}_{\\large b}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n$$\na = 7q, \\qquad b = -5.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax=b$ with $b \\neq 0$ has no solutions when $a = 0.$\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7q = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7q & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7q}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation $7qx = -5$ has no solutions if $q = 0.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204177 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078606 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-61df167c-8856-5b89-8c68-3c6ad59fc5b1" "question-d62032b2-c1a5-5a5d-886c-e5b808c77233" "question-02a39903-e9f1-5373-88d1-5e7a858c1c6d" "question-0089d6b7-bea1-5c5c-b759-cd65107e82f0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078607 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9bc69072-1729-5358-9591-a855b171ce26" "question-8a10d42c-0c01-597c-81cf-42c95eadf6ff" "question-862b2bca-cb58-5918-b28a-220de43ef262"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078608 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5bf64a24-1ed0-5d2f-8ee9-666d256fd892" "question-630319b1-f66a-5f1d-ad45-2673438230d6" "question-e2ff1bdc-524d-5a25-83f2-62b0e84aa209"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-61df167c-8856-5b89-8c68-3c6ad59fc5b1" :question/id #uuid "61df167c-8856-5b89-8c68-3c6ad59fc5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-4809" :question/problem "Given the following data for a function $f(x):$\n\n| **$x$** | $1.1$ | $1.3$ | $1.5$ | $1.7$ | $1.9$ | $2.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $12$ | $15$ | $21$ | $23$ | $24$ | $25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1.7$ using a central difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=1.7.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1.7) & ≈\\frac{f(1.7+0.2)-f(1.7-0.2)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1.9)-f(1.5)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-21}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{0.4} \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6fe4a4bf-9911-5202-9b58-2e8068c15035"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6fe4a4bf-9911-5202-9b58-2e8068c15035" :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe4a4bf-9911-5202-9b58-2e8068c15035" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-105028d2-d6dd-5dcf-a926-5082b7f0367c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4dfca398-b647-5b5d-8ae1-37d9307ce4ac" "answer-ae998191-ec29-5aca-94bf-db5f5f3e0b44" "answer-3b4bea6e-bb16-5316-9214-10da85ada450" "answer-3cdeb52d-d5c4-52b2-84f7-ac0b9a9f49e5" "answer-105028d2-d6dd-5dcf-a926-5082b7f0367c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dfca398-b647-5b5d-8ae1-37d9307ce4ac" :answer/id #uuid "4dfca398-b647-5b5d-8ae1-37d9307ce4ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1.7)≈1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae998191-ec29-5aca-94bf-db5f5f3e0b44" :answer/id #uuid "ae998191-ec29-5aca-94bf-db5f5f3e0b44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1.7)≈4.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b4bea6e-bb16-5316-9214-10da85ada450" :answer/id #uuid "3b4bea6e-bb16-5316-9214-10da85ada450" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1.7)≈5.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cdeb52d-d5c4-52b2-84f7-ac0b9a9f49e5" :answer/id #uuid "3cdeb52d-d5c4-52b2-84f7-ac0b9a9f49e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1.7)≈6.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-105028d2-d6dd-5dcf-a926-5082b7f0367c" :answer/id #uuid "105028d2-d6dd-5dcf-a926-5082b7f0367c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1.7)≈7.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d62032b2-c1a5-5a5d-886c-e5b808c77233" :question/id #uuid "d62032b2-c1a5-5a5d-886c-e5b808c77233" :question/math-academy-id "q-314288" :question/problem "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $0.6$ | $0.8$ | $1$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2.6$ | $1.9$ | $1.5$ | $2.3$ | $5.9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0.8$ using a central difference approximation.\n\n${g}^{′}(0.8)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.8.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(0.8) & ≈\\frac{g(0.8+0.2)-g(0.8-0.2)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(1)-g(0.6)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5-2.6}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1.1}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11}{4} \\\\  & =-2.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9bea8aec-8ac7-571d-93ce-c41382fa0763"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9bea8aec-8ac7-571d-93ce-c41382fa0763" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bea8aec-8ac7-571d-93ce-c41382fa0763" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0ccbeb88-908d-55b3-a125-61b3f46ff08b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ccbeb88-908d-55b3-a125-61b3f46ff08b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ccbeb88-908d-55b3-a125-61b3f46ff08b" :answer/id #uuid "0ccbeb88-908d-55b3-a125-61b3f46ff08b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-02a39903-e9f1-5373-88d1-5e7a858c1c6d" :question/id #uuid "02a39903-e9f1-5373-88d1-5e7a858c1c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-314885" :question/problem "Given that $f(1.9)=4.21$ and $f(2.1)=5.81,$ find an approximation for ${f}^{′}(2).$\n\n${f}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2$ with the step size $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2+0.1)-f(2-0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2.1)-f(1.9)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5.81-4.21}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6}{0.2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea39bfa2-a209-51aa-80a0-fff9f13a2672"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea39bfa2-a209-51aa-80a0-fff9f13a2672" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea39bfa2-a209-51aa-80a0-fff9f13a2672" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3c4547a7-1728-5d73-a024-93e088d07cd1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c4547a7-1728-5d73-a024-93e088d07cd1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c4547a7-1728-5d73-a024-93e088d07cd1" :answer/id #uuid "3c4547a7-1728-5d73-a024-93e088d07cd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0089d6b7-bea1-5c5c-b759-cd65107e82f0" :question/id #uuid "0089d6b7-bea1-5c5c-b759-cd65107e82f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343983" :question/problem "Given that $f(1.25)=0.75$ and $f(1.75)=-0.50,$ find an approximation for ${f}^{′}(1.5).$\n\n${f}^{′}(1.5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=1.5$ with the step size $h=0.25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1.5) & ≈\\frac{f(1.5+0.25)-f(1.5-0.25)}{2(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1.75)-f(1.25)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-0.50-0.75}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1.25)}{0.5} \\\\  & =-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-83cf222e-a725-5f50-9a9c-5106f3f53afc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83cf222e-a725-5f50-9a9c-5106f3f53afc" :answer-field/id #uuid "83cf222e-a725-5f50-9a9c-5106f3f53afc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3098c1b5-fa3f-59d6-b4d0-17b2888a2595" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3098c1b5-fa3f-59d6-b4d0-17b2888a2595"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3098c1b5-fa3f-59d6-b4d0-17b2888a2595" :answer/id #uuid "3098c1b5-fa3f-59d6-b4d0-17b2888a2595" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9bc69072-1729-5358-9591-a855b171ce26" :question/id #uuid "9bc69072-1729-5358-9591-a855b171ce26" :question/math-academy-id "q-344026" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c2d36807b25464536f395912dad0443831f4846f23a337e9495cf673bd488d6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=0$ and $x=2.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=1$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{f(1+1)-f(1-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-45759e68-39d9-53a8-920d-06189f95b4ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-45759e68-39d9-53a8-920d-06189f95b4ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "45759e68-39d9-53a8-920d-06189f95b4ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dd90a7dc-ed12-541e-9465-1c706693c184" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd90a7dc-ed12-541e-9465-1c706693c184"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd90a7dc-ed12-541e-9465-1c706693c184" :answer/id #uuid "dd90a7dc-ed12-541e-9465-1c706693c184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8a10d42c-0c01-597c-81cf-42c95eadf6ff" :question/id #uuid "8a10d42c-0c01-597c-81cf-42c95eadf6ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-344003" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e526d91e13619efd7dfd8cab4143a43a4e0d373ad2766a0aac2de02a8f7ec7f8.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=-4$ and $x=0.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(-2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=-2$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & ≈\\frac{f(-2+2)-f(-2-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0)-f(-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5f57be83-0954-54b9-b4ea-a48c439af8b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5f57be83-0954-54b9-b4ea-a48c439af8b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f57be83-0954-54b9-b4ea-a48c439af8b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6dbd1d74-f9fa-51ba-8016-e774785e51b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6dbd1d74-f9fa-51ba-8016-e774785e51b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dbd1d74-f9fa-51ba-8016-e774785e51b0" :answer/id #uuid "6dbd1d74-f9fa-51ba-8016-e774785e51b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-862b2bca-cb58-5918-b28a-220de43ef262" :question/id #uuid "862b2bca-cb58-5918-b28a-220de43ef262" :question/math-academy-id "q-47533" :question/problem "![](images/52/52d37029cc93ea1e0be1c9a1488aa3275437c4be2b749ff09a497935de8399a4.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+2)-g(4-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b1257117-ddc2-5b07-841b-2f9e8a671fda"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b1257117-ddc2-5b07-841b-2f9e8a671fda" :answer-field/id #uuid "b1257117-ddc2-5b07-841b-2f9e8a671fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f9905493-fe7b-54f0-8777-6376ee8c61c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a7f9747-962b-5ef8-ae18-b6975de0ec0b" "answer-3599af72-f95a-547c-ba9a-fa458f70f570" "answer-391dd3a9-0b1b-5cc6-a2f9-5c1ec302e259" "answer-3380260b-e523-5086-a894-4d8f8bf9fdd0" "answer-f9905493-fe7b-54f0-8777-6376ee8c61c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a7f9747-962b-5ef8-ae18-b6975de0ec0b" :answer/id #uuid "9a7f9747-962b-5ef8-ae18-b6975de0ec0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3599af72-f95a-547c-ba9a-fa458f70f570" :answer/id #uuid "3599af72-f95a-547c-ba9a-fa458f70f570" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-391dd3a9-0b1b-5cc6-a2f9-5c1ec302e259" :answer/id #uuid "391dd3a9-0b1b-5cc6-a2f9-5c1ec302e259" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3380260b-e523-5086-a894-4d8f8bf9fdd0" :answer/id #uuid "3380260b-e523-5086-a894-4d8f8bf9fdd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9905493-fe7b-54f0-8777-6376ee8c61c2" :answer/id #uuid "f9905493-fe7b-54f0-8777-6376ee8c61c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5bf64a24-1ed0-5d2f-8ee9-666d256fd892" :question/id #uuid "5bf64a24-1ed0-5d2f-8ee9-666d256fd892" :question/math-academy-id "q-344364" :question/problem "In a nutrition study, the mass of bacteria in a culture (in $\\text{g}$) after $t$ hours is given by the function $M(t).$ The values of $M(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.5$ | $1.8$ | $2.1$ | $2.4$ | $2.7$ | $3.0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$M(t)$** | $6.2$ | $6.8$ | $7.5$ | $8.1$ | $8.7$ | $9.3$ |\n\nApproximate the bacteria’s mass instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.3.$\n\nThe bacteria’s mass at $t=2.4$ is changing at approximately \n{{field-1}} $g$ per hour." :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{M(t+h)-M(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat time $t=2.4$ with step size $h=0.3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{′}(2.4) & ≈\\frac{M(2.4+0.3)-M(2.4-0.3)}{2(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{M(2.7)-M(2.1)}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8.7-7.5}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.2}{0.6} \\\\  & =2.0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the bacteria’s mass at $t=2.4$ is changing at approximately $2.0\\,g$ per hour." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-369cc8e3-5474-53d6-a060-3bb5cec64cd1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-369cc8e3-5474-53d6-a060-3bb5cec64cd1" :answer-field/id #uuid "369cc8e3-5474-53d6-a060-3bb5cec64cd1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bed2ee92-7821-5be1-89a3-320104da1adb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bed2ee92-7821-5be1-89a3-320104da1adb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bed2ee92-7821-5be1-89a3-320104da1adb" :answer/id #uuid "bed2ee92-7821-5be1-89a3-320104da1adb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-630319b1-f66a-5f1d-ad45-2673438230d6" :question/id #uuid "630319b1-f66a-5f1d-ad45-2673438230d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47498" :question/problem "A car travels on a straight road in such a way that its position, in meters, after $t$ seconds is given by the function $f(t).$ The values of $f(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $14$ | $15$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the velocity ${f}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{f(t+h)-f(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=4$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{f(4+2)-f(4-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(6)-f(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the car at the instant of time $t=4$ is $0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ab13eb1-4f8f-5f20-84da-006bd8395175"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ab13eb1-4f8f-5f20-84da-006bd8395175" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab13eb1-4f8f-5f20-84da-006bd8395175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7370b0a8-d27a-5f48-973e-074b1f8d6139" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7370b0a8-d27a-5f48-973e-074b1f8d6139" "answer-8a0cb2fe-db1d-5364-896a-42e989e126d1" "answer-07b1993e-1478-5444-9608-5ffec37aa265" "answer-e509c827-a594-508a-9b0a-727051003b91" "answer-4641b589-d1df-5109-886b-057522bb0ceb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7370b0a8-d27a-5f48-973e-074b1f8d6139" :answer/id #uuid "7370b0a8-d27a-5f48-973e-074b1f8d6139" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a0cb2fe-db1d-5364-896a-42e989e126d1" :answer/id #uuid "8a0cb2fe-db1d-5364-896a-42e989e126d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07b1993e-1478-5444-9608-5ffec37aa265" :answer/id #uuid "07b1993e-1478-5444-9608-5ffec37aa265" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e509c827-a594-508a-9b0a-727051003b91" :answer/id #uuid "e509c827-a594-508a-9b0a-727051003b91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4641b589-d1df-5109-886b-057522bb0ceb" :answer/id #uuid "4641b589-d1df-5109-886b-057522bb0ceb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2ff1bdc-524d-5a25-83f2-62b0e84aa209" :question/id #uuid "e2ff1bdc-524d-5a25-83f2-62b0e84aa209" :question/math-academy-id "q-51404" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4ede7ee4b2f41d19ba36d8e1cb28e9ab480a54c83310686baa35e7d08a7e7fd4.png)\n\nA liquid is heated to ${150}^{∘}\\text{C}$ and then left to cool. The temperature of the liquid after $t$ hours is given by $T(t).$  The graph of $T(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate of change of temperature when $t=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{T}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{T(t+h)-T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(-1) & ≈\\frac{T(2+1)-T(2-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(3)-T(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{60-120}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-60}{2} \\\\  & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of temperature at the instant of time $t=2$ is $-30{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ per hour." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a16e3a72-88b6-545d-81dc-981c40d41aa2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a16e3a72-88b6-545d-81dc-981c40d41aa2" :answer-field/id #uuid "a16e3a72-88b6-545d-81dc-981c40d41aa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2b483a84-5c97-56c1-906f-fbb9b1f1e3e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5023fca-8043-5cc4-96cc-7e4e7ac32a5e" "answer-65de6908-4c51-5436-83fd-aa3e0f24419f" "answer-2b483a84-5c97-56c1-906f-fbb9b1f1e3e2" "answer-738573b4-cd3c-5ee2-b300-6d4a28ee74b4" "answer-1ee0af75-bcb6-5b60-b0e8-3fd1b8dc76bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5023fca-8043-5cc4-96cc-7e4e7ac32a5e" :answer/id #uuid "f5023fca-8043-5cc4-96cc-7e4e7ac32a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-15{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65de6908-4c51-5436-83fd-aa3e0f24419f" :answer/id #uuid "65de6908-4c51-5436-83fd-aa3e0f24419f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b483a84-5c97-56c1-906f-fbb9b1f1e3e2" :answer/id #uuid "2b483a84-5c97-56c1-906f-fbb9b1f1e3e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-30{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-738573b4-cd3c-5ee2-b300-6d4a28ee74b4" :answer/id #uuid "738573b4-cd3c-5ee2-b300-6d4a28ee74b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-45{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ee0af75-bcb6-5b60-b0e8-3fd1b8dc76bb" :answer/id #uuid "1ee0af75-bcb6-5b60-b0e8-3fd1b8dc76bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-50{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ per hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078662 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" "question-b8f3b4b6-eb37-510a-a5f6-48850ac9d684"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :question/id #uuid "0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :question/math-academy-id "q-90392" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}},\\,x>1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" "answer-67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" "answer-1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" "answer-13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" "answer-8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" :answer/id #uuid "7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" :answer/id #uuid "67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" :answer/id #uuid "13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c" :answer/id #uuid "8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b8f3b4b6-eb37-510a-a5f6-48850ac9d684" :question/id #uuid "b8f3b4b6-eb37-510a-a5f6-48850ac9d684" :question/math-academy-id "q-131140" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,1<x<e\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=e-1,$ compute $\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X]=2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=e-1$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-X] & =2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}-(e-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1-e+1 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-440eb13c-81c4-5c5d-9faa-611d1e590780"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-440eb13c-81c4-5c5d-9faa-611d1e590780" :answer-field/id #uuid "440eb13c-81c4-5c5d-9faa-611d1e590780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bfd68903-0ef9-5e2a-88ed-3677ac3ae07b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c01cb739-f7ab-5ebb-8e34-daf477537efd" "answer-b269375e-588f-5e5f-9ae9-591c8a3221c9" "answer-2b2368f6-3f68-54b0-9d32-a27cbf3895be" "answer-b02c68e4-e61b-5654-9879-70de70a8fe6d" "answer-bfd68903-0ef9-5e2a-88ed-3677ac3ae07b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c01cb739-f7ab-5ebb-8e34-daf477537efd" :answer/id #uuid "c01cb739-f7ab-5ebb-8e34-daf477537efd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-5e+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b269375e-588f-5e5f-9ae9-591c8a3221c9" :answer/id #uuid "b269375e-588f-5e5f-9ae9-591c8a3221c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b2368f6-3f68-54b0-9d32-a27cbf3895be" :answer/id #uuid "2b2368f6-3f68-54b0-9d32-a27cbf3895be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b02c68e4-e61b-5654-9879-70de70a8fe6d" :answer/id #uuid "b02c68e4-e61b-5654-9879-70de70a8fe6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2e+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfd68903-0ef9-5e2a-88ed-3677ac3ae07b" :answer/id #uuid "bfd68903-0ef9-5e2a-88ed-3677ac3ae07b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-3d4cf227-5451-5675-a3d0-310e1e202b0c" :activity/id #uuid "3d4cf227-5451-5675-a3d0-310e1e202b0c" :activity/title "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048747 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-a28c654f-55ef-5916-869c-70e534152c19" "step-81573813-f419-5bab-b167-8255dfe1a85b" "step-b234828c-5807-5de4-a2a5-5f231ccf4ede" "step-62dbfd8d-d6c8-5022-be2b-1ea1fd60fadf" "step-0aef0e95-7b36-56cc-a917-a6909d024646"] :activity/first-step "step-a28c654f-55ef-5916-869c-70e534152c19" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a28c654f-55ef-5916-869c-70e534152c19" :step/id #uuid "a28c654f-55ef-5916-869c-70e534152c19" :step/content "question-0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :step/next "step-81573813-f419-5bab-b167-8255dfe1a85b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-81573813-f419-5bab-b167-8255dfe1a85b" :step/id #uuid "81573813-f419-5bab-b167-8255dfe1a85b" :step/content "question-b8f3b4b6-eb37-510a-a5f6-48850ac9d684" :step/next "step-b234828c-5807-5de4-a2a5-5f231ccf4ede" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b234828c-5807-5de4-a2a5-5f231ccf4ede" :step/id #uuid "b234828c-5807-5de4-a2a5-5f231ccf4ede" :step/content 17592186434427 :step/next "step-62dbfd8d-d6c8-5022-be2b-1ea1fd60fadf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-62dbfd8d-d6c8-5022-be2b-1ea1fd60fadf" :step/id #uuid "62dbfd8d-d6c8-5022-be2b-1ea1fd60fadf" :step/content 17592186479115 :step/next "step-0aef0e95-7b36-56cc-a917-a6909d024646" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0aef0e95-7b36-56cc-a917-a6909d024646" :step/id #uuid "0aef0e95-7b36-56cc-a917-a6909d024646" :step/content 17592186434426 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186098685 :answer-field/choices 17592186098689] {:db/id 17592186100811 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2656d5b-6dc2-54fa-8fec-eaac03937257"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101224 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=7.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=7$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)}{{7}^{2}\\cdot g(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{49\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x))^{′}}{({x}^{2}g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ again, and this time, we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(7)}{2\\cdot 7\\cdot g(7)+{7}^{2}\\cdot {g}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+49\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{98}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}=\\frac{1}{98}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098679 :answer-field/correct 17592186098683 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100812 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-04a061b7-e5c9-583d-9ee4-5ed73b8719bb" "question-8771f8a8-d420-525c-a30c-b75746b0e1ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101225 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=10.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-20$ | $-2$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=10$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)+5}{20+g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+5}{20+(-20)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{(f(x)+5)^{′}}{(2x+g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(10)}{2+{g}^{′}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x))^{′}}{(2+{g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=10,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(10)}{{g}^{″}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098685 :answer-field/correct 17592186098686 :answer-field/choices ["answer-0351928c-3e28-54f2-8865-ccfc512917a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0351928c-3e28-54f2-8865-ccfc512917a4" :answer/id #uuid "0351928c-3e28-54f2-8865-ccfc512917a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2656d5b-6dc2-54fa-8fec-eaac03937257" :question/id #uuid "f2656d5b-6dc2-54fa-8fec-eaac03937257" :question/math-academy-id "q-337001" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=2.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=2$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3} & ={\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3}|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+g(2)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(2)+2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{5}}{1+2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5})^{′}}{(f(x)+x-3)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(x)}{2\\sqrt{x+g(x)}}}{{f}^{′}(x)+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2$ again, and this time, we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(x)}{2\\sqrt{x+g(x)}}}{{f}^{′}(x)+1} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(x)}{2\\sqrt{x+g(x)}}}{{f}^{′}(x)+1}|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(2)}{2\\sqrt{2+g(2)}}}{{f}^{′}(2)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1+4}{2\\sqrt{5}}}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-45f70f8d-7947-54a7-beae-a31e706e6bd8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-45f70f8d-7947-54a7-beae-a31e706e6bd8" :answer-field/id #uuid "45f70f8d-7947-54a7-beae-a31e706e6bd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9c7e66c8-4fa9-5767-b29b-481d9fe42d85" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c7e66c8-4fa9-5767-b29b-481d9fe42d85"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c7e66c8-4fa9-5767-b29b-481d9fe42d85" :answer/id #uuid "9c7e66c8-4fa9-5767-b29b-481d9fe42d85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04a061b7-e5c9-583d-9ee4-5ed73b8719bb" :question/id #uuid "04a061b7-e5c9-583d-9ee4-5ed73b8719bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-337014" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $e$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x\\,f(x)}{\\,f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}\\,}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-xf(x)}{f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}} & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}-xf(x)}{f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-1\\cdot f(1)}{f(1)g(1)-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-e}{(e)(1)-e} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-xf(x)}{f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({e}^{x}-xf(x))^{′}}{(f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x))}{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, and once more we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x))}{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x}}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-(f(1)+1\\cdot {f}^{′}(1))}{{f}^{′}(1)g(1)+f(1){g}^{′}(1)-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-(e+0)}{(0)(1)+(e)(1)-e} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x))}{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x)))^{′}}{({f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-(2{f}^{′}(x)+x{f}^{″}(x))}{{f}^{″}(x)g(x)+2{f}^{′}(x){g}^{′}(x)+f(x){g}^{″}(x)-{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute $x=1$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{e}^{x}-(2{f}^{′}(x)+x{f}^{″}(x))}{{f}^{″}(x)g(x)+2{f}^{′}(x){g}^{′}(x)+f(x){g}^{″}(x)-{e}^{x}}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-(2{f}^{′}(1)+1\\cdot {f}^{″}(1))}{{f}^{″}(1)g(1)+2{f}^{′}(1){g}^{′}(1)+f(1){g}^{″}(1)-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-(2(0)+0)}{(0)(1)+2(0)(1)+(e)(2)-e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{e} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x\\,f(x)}{\\,f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}\\,}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4aaa0726-be5f-51d0-b8c1-e8e9cc273212"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4aaa0726-be5f-51d0-b8c1-e8e9cc273212" :answer-field/id #uuid "4aaa0726-be5f-51d0-b8c1-e8e9cc273212" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cdf52d1c-d0cf-596a-b9d3-a36801c3cff4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cdf52d1c-d0cf-596a-b9d3-a36801c3cff4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdf52d1c-d0cf-596a-b9d3-a36801c3cff4" :answer/id #uuid "cdf52d1c-d0cf-596a-b9d3-a36801c3cff4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8771f8a8-d420-525c-a30c-b75746b0e1ac" :question/id #uuid "8771f8a8-d420-525c-a30c-b75746b0e1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-337304" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\,f(x)+{x}^{2}-3\\,}{\\,g(x)+{x}^{2}-4\\,}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+{x}^{2}-3}{g(x)+{x}^{2}-4} & ={\\frac{f(x)+{x}^{2}-3}{g(x)+{x}^{2}-4}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+{1}^{2}-3}{g(1)+{1}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+1-3}{3+1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+{x}^{2}-3}{g(x)+{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+{x}^{2}-3)^{′}}{(g(x)+{x}^{2}-4)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+2x}{{g}^{′}(x)+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, and once more we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+2x}{{g}^{′}(x)+2x}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)+2(1)}{{g}^{′}(1)+2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+2x}{{g}^{′}(x)+2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x)+2x)^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x)+2x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)+2}{{g}^{″}(x)+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute $x=1$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)+2}{{g}^{″}(x)+2}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)+2}{{g}^{″}(1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\,f(x)+{x}^{2}-3\\,}{\\,g(x)+{x}^{2}-4\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02cd3c44-7134-56fe-b1d4-7d91f48f8a31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02cd3c44-7134-56fe-b1d4-7d91f48f8a31" :answer-field/id #uuid "02cd3c44-7134-56fe-b1d4-7d91f48f8a31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-52ad2c6b-d809-5214-a3b9-4020cbd24ed3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52ad2c6b-d809-5214-a3b9-4020cbd24ed3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52ad2c6b-d809-5214-a3b9-4020cbd24ed3" :answer/id #uuid "52ad2c6b-d809-5214-a3b9-4020cbd24ed3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-14ab7d13-6f0b-5222-a3a9-1ed470e14850" :activity/id #uuid "14ab7d13-6f0b-5222-a3a9-1ed470e14850" :activity/title "L'Hopital's Rule Applied to Tables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050821 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-e4ad71be-bbfe-51a4-8088-8a49f9968f26" "step-a19df4dd-8442-5969-9c7a-12325b241bcc" "step-583d5166-dcc0-5b93-bda9-b993bee6d50d" "step-d7f3584e-f4ec-53ca-ac99-2039e2a0c5b7" "step-b87c4769-3b0d-596e-be1a-a6f3c4ee5c2e"] :activity/first-step "step-e4ad71be-bbfe-51a4-8088-8a49f9968f26" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-e4ad71be-bbfe-51a4-8088-8a49f9968f26" :step/id #uuid "e4ad71be-bbfe-51a4-8088-8a49f9968f26" :step/content 17592186101224 :step/next "step-a19df4dd-8442-5969-9c7a-12325b241bcc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a19df4dd-8442-5969-9c7a-12325b241bcc" :step/id #uuid "a19df4dd-8442-5969-9c7a-12325b241bcc" :step/content 17592186101225 :step/next "step-583d5166-dcc0-5b93-bda9-b993bee6d50d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-583d5166-dcc0-5b93-bda9-b993bee6d50d" :step/id #uuid "583d5166-dcc0-5b93-bda9-b993bee6d50d" :step/content "question-f2656d5b-6dc2-54fa-8fec-eaac03937257" :step/next "step-d7f3584e-f4ec-53ca-ac99-2039e2a0c5b7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d7f3584e-f4ec-53ca-ac99-2039e2a0c5b7" :step/id #uuid "d7f3584e-f4ec-53ca-ac99-2039e2a0c5b7" :step/content "question-04a061b7-e5c9-583d-9ee4-5ed73b8719bb" :step/next "step-b87c4769-3b0d-596e-be1a-a6f3c4ee5c2e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b87c4769-3b0d-596e-be1a-a6f3c4ee5c2e" :step/id #uuid "b87c4769-3b0d-596e-be1a-a6f3c4ee5c2e" :step/content "question-8771f8a8-d420-525c-a30c-b75746b0e1ac" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157770 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =x\\cdot {q}_{3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4{x}_{3}=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155541 :answer-field/correct 17592186155542 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351220 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $20{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351221 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $10{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351235 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\sqrt{6}{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186373449 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $-2{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c1103ca1-a4b8-5e45-80ee-206e2a1e5539" :activity/id #uuid "c1103ca1-a4b8-5e45-80ee-206e2a1e5539" :activity/title "The Components of a Vector with Respect to an Orthogonal or Orthonormal Basis" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050366 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-b1a31c7e-0951-5165-83b7-3c5aa3c9dc20" "step-9bc5e018-a1be-5fe9-a7e1-d943f160ff64" "step-df9bf2a8-1619-5624-a3e0-651ea74d1dcb" "step-d60fa14c-dd43-51e9-b0de-5ea532d37c12" "step-3d2d4703-0f66-5735-a395-f8a1316dde9f"] :activity/first-step "step-b1a31c7e-0951-5165-83b7-3c5aa3c9dc20" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1a31c7e-0951-5165-83b7-3c5aa3c9dc20" :step/id #uuid "b1a31c7e-0951-5165-83b7-3c5aa3c9dc20" :step/content 17592186157770 :step/next "step-9bc5e018-a1be-5fe9-a7e1-d943f160ff64" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9bc5e018-a1be-5fe9-a7e1-d943f160ff64" :step/id #uuid "9bc5e018-a1be-5fe9-a7e1-d943f160ff64" :step/content 17592186351220 :step/next "step-df9bf2a8-1619-5624-a3e0-651ea74d1dcb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-df9bf2a8-1619-5624-a3e0-651ea74d1dcb" :step/id #uuid "df9bf2a8-1619-5624-a3e0-651ea74d1dcb" :step/content 17592186351221 :step/next "step-d60fa14c-dd43-51e9-b0de-5ea532d37c12" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d60fa14c-dd43-51e9-b0de-5ea532d37c12" :step/id #uuid "d60fa14c-dd43-51e9-b0de-5ea532d37c12" :step/content 17592186351235 :step/next "step-3d2d4703-0f66-5735-a395-f8a1316dde9f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3d2d4703-0f66-5735-a395-f8a1316dde9f" :step/id #uuid "3d2d4703-0f66-5735-a395-f8a1316dde9f" :step/content 17592186373449 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110736 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d8c01898-d5f3-593c-be7d-e90b6733c0c5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110370 :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-1} =4.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $\\left( -1, 4\\right).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/4c/4cbb5007cf380afe964bea8b5970e4a37924f38401ec24cb856b02c0510676eb.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9fbf8c06-659f-590c-beea-a6431f0cb137" "question-94e841e5-3216-5691-bf5f-488fa6567b77"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110313 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = (0.2)^x?$\n\nI. The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $y\\gt 0.$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $\\left( 1,0.2\\right).$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x) = (0.2)^x$ is defined for all input values $x,$ and, the set of possible outputs consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(1) = (0.2)^1 = 0.2 .\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ decreases, the function value gets larger and larger, approaching *positive* infinity. Therefore $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5e3ff34b-d5cf-5bf6-ba34-eb80d066dc38" "question-733833e2-6c11-5af9-9fa6-cb304f5e9297"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110314 :question/problem "Plot the graph of ![](images/ee/ee7c536e7564d206978b4714c551e52f72bcd604881d7a9c64d2ca352432d060.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of ![](images/05/05e47fd146d7d132ae3688982e5f30c0f592790360a98b13f1d13b66ae53c645.svg)\n\n![](images/01/01d329ac60c1a70c38fc89d8d679879f8e584cd4eb9d55412a3c43d6d43c8129.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of ![](images/92/92a113026443c67e5e8a7680835f0ae448b4a2c8ba773421e478cafe1c638f6f.svg) The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept becomes ![](images/90/900a9e2e74d5210f297c674edf09e4f42d338f866eaa61db18ac8c4047ebfbd8.svg) and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/e6/e6858bdc1668b19f76369796febbe89208bc98a6f553737db87421d4920f35c3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110315 :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^x.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n![](images/24/247e8b4712be05f38cfba373d930c5c8ee65ab64c9619e4ee74ec488fbd0c61d.png)" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k\\left(\\dfrac25\\right)^x$ we take the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)^x$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=1.5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111140 :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=(0.6)^{-1}=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,\\frac{5}{3}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c5bf6531bf352da158771f92eaab5a3974ccd623d1bf29dbe3ecba287f61d5d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186108575 :answer-field/correct 17592186108576 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-d8c01898-d5f3-593c-be7d-e90b6733c0c5" :question/id #uuid "d8c01898-d5f3-593c-be7d-e90b6733c0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-29397" :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{-1}=5.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,5).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f06659c0-7b4f-5aa8-8396-f34d621a1627"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f06659c0-7b4f-5aa8-8396-f34d621a1627" :answer-field/id #uuid "f06659c0-7b4f-5aa8-8396-f34d621a1627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" "answer-d2e41aa6-94d0-5182-b006-2e6cf57099a7" "answer-ff96ccd6-24f2-5ea1-8f70-ebd65b0d2c91" "answer-2fa46daf-b83a-59a7-a072-244a33736204" "answer-2494f4ae-d21d-5b05-9615-a60fc623cee7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" :answer/id #uuid "1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2e41aa6-94d0-5182-b006-2e6cf57099a7" :answer/id #uuid "d2e41aa6-94d0-5182-b006-2e6cf57099a7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/28/286752cfbadff12d52d940b193f37cf4f4fade6da05f239f350905ce128ebabf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff96ccd6-24f2-5ea1-8f70-ebd65b0d2c91" :answer/id #uuid "ff96ccd6-24f2-5ea1-8f70-ebd65b0d2c91" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/48/48a219b02ff28ff978dea320428b3afbc6f6cae3b9265b2c21c471ee751e542f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fa46daf-b83a-59a7-a072-244a33736204" :answer/id #uuid "2fa46daf-b83a-59a7-a072-244a33736204" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b8/b843d7fa9628622fc74ea9989fddb8b459952369c6bd6b4e9cecd754cb4acf0b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2494f4ae-d21d-5b05-9615-a60fc623cee7" :answer/id #uuid "2494f4ae-d21d-5b05-9615-a60fc623cee7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/bc/bc226e70ed1cd19eca5cca6d82d6e72d4fa1ccd6f243aa7feca9134c11b93ba1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9fbf8c06-659f-590c-beea-a6431f0cb137" :question/id #uuid "9fbf8c06-659f-590c-beea-a6431f0cb137" :question/math-academy-id "q-47746" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(-1,4).$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/5d/5d97ca13c1289558c6bb8e8ce50fb1ef5745f54acf27346a32d6451b1bb7f9bb.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-1)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5aba8ef7-d5cb-5b87-8948-2023f65a58ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5aba8ef7-d5cb-5b87-8948-2023f65a58ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "5aba8ef7-d5cb-5b87-8948-2023f65a58ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70f163bc-40df-5709-9347-836972090890" "answer-e6a08e1c-63f5-575a-8ab3-1f91c228a8cb" "answer-3ee41f75-75e8-560d-a0d8-a810a881a517" "answer-b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" "answer-b4db0f16-b037-5c85-9197-78dadf2c5e6a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70f163bc-40df-5709-9347-836972090890" :answer/id #uuid "70f163bc-40df-5709-9347-836972090890" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6a08e1c-63f5-575a-8ab3-1f91c228a8cb" :answer/id #uuid "e6a08e1c-63f5-575a-8ab3-1f91c228a8cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ee41f75-75e8-560d-a0d8-a810a881a517" :answer/id #uuid "3ee41f75-75e8-560d-a0d8-a810a881a517" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" :answer/id #uuid "b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4db0f16-b037-5c85-9197-78dadf2c5e6a" :answer/id #uuid "b4db0f16-b037-5c85-9197-78dadf2c5e6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-94e841e5-3216-5691-bf5f-488fa6567b77" :question/id #uuid "94e841e5-3216-5691-bf5f-488fa6567b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-47731" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its range is $y>\\frac{4}{5}$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/82/8250b7e30d7960f7c06d0e254e8bad97546e959be1ad3128eb491800b6e91533.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is  $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d60905be-5479-5411-b5d1-1ac224418f27"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d60905be-5479-5411-b5d1-1ac224418f27" :answer-field/id #uuid "d60905be-5479-5411-b5d1-1ac224418f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b850d8c-4056-5cb7-8709-ad0f4ffda5d5" "answer-f6fdd93e-817a-5ad8-aca9-7e39b7a8f224" "answer-4cb16e5f-ba42-5bd9-90eb-f56bf17bbb1d" "answer-803213b4-62ce-516b-810e-b7062290768e" "answer-069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b850d8c-4056-5cb7-8709-ad0f4ffda5d5" :answer/id #uuid "9b850d8c-4056-5cb7-8709-ad0f4ffda5d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6fdd93e-817a-5ad8-aca9-7e39b7a8f224" :answer/id #uuid "f6fdd93e-817a-5ad8-aca9-7e39b7a8f224" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cb16e5f-ba42-5bd9-90eb-f56bf17bbb1d" :answer/id #uuid "4cb16e5f-ba42-5bd9-90eb-f56bf17bbb1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-803213b4-62ce-516b-810e-b7062290768e" :answer/id #uuid "803213b4-62ce-516b-810e-b7062290768e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5" :answer/id #uuid "069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e3ff34b-d5cf-5bf6-ba34-eb80d066dc38" :question/id #uuid "5e3ff34b-d5cf-5bf6-ba34-eb80d066dc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-27525" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a5/a5b088c08da85c389cb96add374186a898793ec23cf0a3e39270c292fe3d606d.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{4}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d563be7169471498c6e0193e3be8493ae6ba119fc3fc7b74b397454b65e6cf57.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6d8c285-32aa-5c3d-ae1d-8f1ac9cad4c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6d8c285-32aa-5c3d-ae1d-8f1ac9cad4c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d8c285-32aa-5c3d-ae1d-8f1ac9cad4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1d33b258-c127-5e48-89c1-172347927c06" "answer-2e2779e9-a749-57a4-aa3d-9cd1888dce16" "answer-fe180236-0e5b-5dc5-967b-2a36380c113d" "answer-43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" "answer-e23c2923-cf2d-5e5e-9c53-dfb0399ef7c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d33b258-c127-5e48-89c1-172347927c06" :answer/id #uuid "1d33b258-c127-5e48-89c1-172347927c06" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1a/1abbfb9c3aeaaeed74af4948b0c645384d6f20c04c23d435224b855cf1192e6f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e2779e9-a749-57a4-aa3d-9cd1888dce16" :answer/id #uuid "2e2779e9-a749-57a4-aa3d-9cd1888dce16" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1e/1e5ea7ddfefb6f1ef81e75c123c528327618ca73bc01df764d2cf3e9564835e1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe180236-0e5b-5dc5-967b-2a36380c113d" :answer/id #uuid "fe180236-0e5b-5dc5-967b-2a36380c113d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/54/5430d057eaa68b600ceeecca978212314972653382754767470cca5115c0dca5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer/id #uuid "43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/bb/bb5ac2100e6106e62b930a9a0cb94d8ce315dc41c4e15a40c5e02f5c5cacfa82.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e23c2923-cf2d-5e5e-9c53-dfb0399ef7c1" :answer/id #uuid "e23c2923-cf2d-5e5e-9c53-dfb0399ef7c1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e7/e7e697fe18c032e596590c68a04f0b01893b44ade18c6fcc732a79bf12ebcf4a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-733833e2-6c11-5af9-9fa6-cb304f5e9297" :question/id #uuid "733833e2-6c11-5af9-9fa6-cb304f5e9297" :question/math-academy-id "q-27531" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/d3/d379a1509beb5b611374d49449e23c386c78d20764ddadfd1b28fc2a6964a818.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,4),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/48/48b94fdedbc1f66e8b018b5fafb7e2ee04d5c1b9c558c1e1de42f4d6f3428c64.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab8c54f1-ac78-59cd-9250-a8538cb853a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab8c54f1-ac78-59cd-9250-a8538cb853a7" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8c54f1-ac78-59cd-9250-a8538cb853a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c3b6b136-4626-540b-bc21-4bbc2d52ae54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89b0b47c-97bc-5ce1-a901-60ba28570aea" "answer-36f81b7b-95bc-5070-8586-1cb395b236dd" "answer-91c70715-5f59-54ca-ab8d-95c8c925b87f" "answer-3f66385e-14cc-547e-ba13-182e0f71ee18" "answer-c3b6b136-4626-540b-bc21-4bbc2d52ae54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89b0b47c-97bc-5ce1-a901-60ba28570aea" :answer/id #uuid "89b0b47c-97bc-5ce1-a901-60ba28570aea" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/14/1448fe064fcd0ee80b02f6370fdc79be41d3ad655b06738876c5c8ff0c1363bf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36f81b7b-95bc-5070-8586-1cb395b236dd" :answer/id #uuid "36f81b7b-95bc-5070-8586-1cb395b236dd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/84/84edd950272f87c19041041fdb5175dde4bd04ed62b71be16669c76d00d93b38.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91c70715-5f59-54ca-ab8d-95c8c925b87f" :answer/id #uuid "91c70715-5f59-54ca-ab8d-95c8c925b87f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d6/d620960e531620c0f2fb0ca3d171a6c0d0a6c45348eca914345cd40fd4675245.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f66385e-14cc-547e-ba13-182e0f71ee18" :answer/id #uuid "3f66385e-14cc-547e-ba13-182e0f71ee18" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f3/f373cb57189451d27eaed320639d5fdcb8a1fcf6f4b1b2087acc94b6d719e09c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3b6b136-4626-540b-bc21-4bbc2d52ae54" :answer/id #uuid "c3b6b136-4626-540b-bc21-4bbc2d52ae54" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f6/f6f93982fecfb664cfcc41a849ce14e5da96c98cc59862cf4a859b5c6e766302.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111827 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of ![](images/d0/d0a7418e3803197d07b85f03b206b83bed1c4d451397ae953b05634db9a9f829.svg) First, we create a table of values: \n\n| ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) | ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) | ![](images/63/63df422d619ca962ca5a69bc7d25dd8831269b92fa5d641dcb960819d3868a9a.svg) | ![](images/66/66ef4b5796888b4025ee2d8b3d516ec80741b341db9389696b6e77a4af5208d8.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) | ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/e5/e51853c53dfd3d72f8fd380044f7ce864974b3d8c1ca0e690b7caba34fe3456c.svg) | ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) | ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/0b/0b47cf9f83219ef6bab2ac82c70ded537ce9db2ab3945b85d5ca678f16b30a75.svg) | ![](images/fc/fce859f2a3c5348530c75d1a07cf8bb4ff0a228bdf8d1e493294b4489b84fdee.svg) | ![](images/8a/8ab6a43799989375f441258e25082fb84a876ad9273de9dba1bc200dca195381.svg) |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/b0/b045833e2e37e2ed4c35e9c8abe8c379dfd7de7eded17d4dc943205677d7fce5.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept at ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n2\\. The graph is decreasing.\n\n3\\. For ![](images/e4/e45cbcbc2e3f466cab4e379776bce2640286bc2a918ff0177c0433b97f7cd4b7.svg), the graph grows very rapidly as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) decreases. Symbolically, we can write ![](images/f5/f54328d6cf80c554acae5f7a9693b0eb4ea745ec0a4b0f5013b03a3e41ae27db.svg) as ![](images/72/7248bd0778a7746f6b1018c7bc926d8e678ba829b741beed092d802bd7ba1233.svg)\n\n4\\. For ![](images/ee/ee98d74fc058e95e72d13a5e575e70e4d5adedd26413e3f01ab1e3f788be87e8.svg), the graph gets very close to zero as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) increases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has a horizontal asymptote ![](images/6c/6c63827e94b6c11a9803605bd54156d90d3fcc6ff4639dd179a5fb024bcffaa1.svg) Symbolically, we can write ![](images/43/43a6d025efce6b330ba7907d1daa54ad58cf04feee07adedad66d4c806b5f88d.svg) as ![](images/f9/f9b318487299434d45096742eaad8ea148abb88c27b718c1bc0983f927dc9911.svg)\n\n5\\. The domain is ![](images/04/043681a7084203dcb8609f78989cadb8f0bcd701978361940d93ef850da95a14.svg), and the range is ![](images/95/95d0bc38cd8737723521d2a1846cb71a0dba33d3df26bd73c18acbb650fc8b95.svg)\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve ![](images/90/90517bb7bc12cfec9d00d88592bbda7572f4a0c5cbcf4f66049311d42f20f6ca.svg) with ![](images/e2/e276af13a21df7f4755de7b18da1a10ca590bbdf3387515efa0896f6cddb79cf.svg) These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8e7cc0db94f5f51bc83404351a7708171fdce50e2946b51263885e4230ecef.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111828 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/d5/d59881254ee1a66445dcf2697e8af4426cbe9d219443d5f40ef2aefc426d598b.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\nIt takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/d5/d5cf96822f65e51d618b651174f1c516eb97be3bf61f5b1380d1c245d29a5a72.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n![](images/99/99f750a011acdbe86e9c9397d5e5901c686a2c10b625fa9dc98a0b90a516c592.svg)\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n![](images/c8/c8c077b168d3c60c74b354ee942893abefb51b5e08b7483044ea5ea2edf50795.svg)\nby a factor of ![](images/31/31659384627c9aa2540de6fca01634acdcdd4c77dd705fbf922c813cc23e822f.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/c8/c8c077b168d3c60c74b354ee942893abefb51b5e08b7483044ea5ea2edf50795.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/31/31659384627c9aa2540de6fca01634acdcdd4c77dd705fbf922c813cc23e822f.svg) This has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110876 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-462f5046-e4ee-5f94-8bfd-00082400022c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-462f5046-e4ee-5f94-8bfd-00082400022c" :question/id #uuid "462f5046-e4ee-5f94-8bfd-00082400022c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74956" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1) & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}+1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44b72334-231e-5d57-bdc0-d2fb9585f9c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44b72334-231e-5d57-bdc0-d2fb9585f9c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "44b72334-231e-5d57-bdc0-d2fb9585f9c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7fd6bc83-ee8d-5527-acf0-39f4c02d1429" :answer-field/choices ["answer-acdc3a68-84af-5610-9099-7c787b10a4a1" "answer-8f99aa38-d301-56e1-857e-d573e65c98ad" "answer-1dc3fc2e-8291-595d-9efe-37b54c54ec70" "answer-7fd6bc83-ee8d-5527-acf0-39f4c02d1429" "answer-21b545b6-d2ce-5675-b1f3-a77567c762a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acdc3a68-84af-5610-9099-7c787b10a4a1" :answer/id #uuid "acdc3a68-84af-5610-9099-7c787b10a4a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f99aa38-d301-56e1-857e-d573e65c98ad" :answer/id #uuid "8f99aa38-d301-56e1-857e-d573e65c98ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dc3fc2e-8291-595d-9efe-37b54c54ec70" :answer/id #uuid "1dc3fc2e-8291-595d-9efe-37b54c54ec70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fd6bc83-ee8d-5527-acf0-39f4c02d1429" :answer/id #uuid "7fd6bc83-ee8d-5527-acf0-39f4c02d1429" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21b545b6-d2ce-5675-b1f3-a77567c762a5" :answer/id #uuid "21b545b6-d2ce-5675-b1f3-a77567c762a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6b2bedc8-52f9-5d40-afcd-8617235afd0f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b2bedc8-52f9-5d40-afcd-8617235afd0f" :question/id #uuid "6b2bedc8-52f9-5d40-afcd-8617235afd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129401" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(1,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,x=1}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f962e375-138e-5ba3-a11f-61cf507ac9c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f962e375-138e-5ba3-a11f-61cf507ac9c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "f962e375-138e-5ba3-a11f-61cf507ac9c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5beae258-194a-5396-abb9-d22777307972" :answer-field/choices ["answer-824d43de-2c6c-5256-ab7d-e1e81ca546b1" "answer-15c9e982-2244-5a23-aac9-ea8f242dfd59" "answer-2c45373f-5f36-5270-9c27-ce7558fa56c2" "answer-59f30dc1-5f5d-5fdc-a739-06dad6b23c63" "answer-5beae258-194a-5396-abb9-d22777307972"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-824d43de-2c6c-5256-ab7d-e1e81ca546b1" :answer/id #uuid "824d43de-2c6c-5256-ab7d-e1e81ca546b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15c9e982-2244-5a23-aac9-ea8f242dfd59" :answer/id #uuid "15c9e982-2244-5a23-aac9-ea8f242dfd59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c45373f-5f36-5270-9c27-ce7558fa56c2" :answer/id #uuid "2c45373f-5f36-5270-9c27-ce7558fa56c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59f30dc1-5f5d-5fdc-a739-06dad6b23c63" :answer/id #uuid "59f30dc1-5f5d-5fdc-a739-06dad6b23c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5beae258-194a-5396-abb9-d22777307972" :answer/id #uuid "5beae258-194a-5396-abb9-d22777307972" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ceebe888-3e8e-5cd4-adb8-cd12e96beae4" :activity/id #uuid "ceebe888-3e8e-5cd4-adb8-cd12e96beae4" :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048449 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-3c399357-4d7f-55ba-a5f1-d7571818aae3" "step-3074aaf8-a64d-58be-886f-5f8b626d0bc8" "step-7b2bc548-78f2-5e4e-b985-befdf65ed49c" "step-33d52943-cf7c-5ddf-bb1f-113936cfa6d5" "step-ee66d94f-00d8-5e5e-84ff-35a7175f7dff"] :activity/first-step "step-3c399357-4d7f-55ba-a5f1-d7571818aae3" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3c399357-4d7f-55ba-a5f1-d7571818aae3" :step/id #uuid "3c399357-4d7f-55ba-a5f1-d7571818aae3" :step/content 17592186344994 :step/next "step-3074aaf8-a64d-58be-886f-5f8b626d0bc8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3074aaf8-a64d-58be-886f-5f8b626d0bc8" :step/id #uuid "3074aaf8-a64d-58be-886f-5f8b626d0bc8" :step/content "question-462f5046-e4ee-5f94-8bfd-00082400022c" :step/next "step-7b2bc548-78f2-5e4e-b985-befdf65ed49c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7b2bc548-78f2-5e4e-b985-befdf65ed49c" :step/id #uuid "7b2bc548-78f2-5e4e-b985-befdf65ed49c" :step/content 17592186407846 :step/next "step-33d52943-cf7c-5ddf-bb1f-113936cfa6d5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33d52943-cf7c-5ddf-bb1f-113936cfa6d5" :step/id #uuid "33d52943-cf7c-5ddf-bb1f-113936cfa6d5" :step/content 17592186265716 :step/next "step-ee66d94f-00d8-5e5e-84ff-35a7175f7dff" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ee66d94f-00d8-5e5e-84ff-35a7175f7dff" :step/id #uuid "ee66d94f-00d8-5e5e-84ff-35a7175f7dff" :step/content "question-6b2bedc8-52f9-5d40-afcd-8617235afd0f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235412 :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235181 :answer-field/correct "answer-7bb5d9a6-29d0-523a-bf1e-a8d90f76c3e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7bb5d9a6-29d0-523a-bf1e-a8d90f76c3e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bb5d9a6-29d0-523a-bf1e-a8d90f76c3e3" :answer/id #uuid "7bb5d9a6-29d0-523a-bf1e-a8d90f76c3e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235413 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}{f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235012 :answer-field/correct "answer-a0781cd4-b883-570b-8c0c-22df23deadc9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0781cd4-b883-570b-8c0c-22df23deadc9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0781cd4-b883-570b-8c0c-22df23deadc9" :answer/id #uuid "a0781cd4-b883-570b-8c0c-22df23deadc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235414 :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n${f}^{′}(2)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)-f(2)}{x-2}=-{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}} \n$).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235415 :question/problem "$-{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\,(x+2)=${{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235416 :question/problem "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}($\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,h\\,).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235417 :question/problem "${f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(-2x-h)=${{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235418 :question/problem "![](images/19/19d0c50eff5613451c833c00e2d151b93ca5d7e490366fc811d6730e383227de.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above (not drawn to scale). Estimate the area $A$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n$A\\,≈\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235419 :question/problem "Write the expression for the Riemann sum from part 7 in sigma notation.\n\nThe right Riemann sum $\\,=\\,0.5{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}(4-$  \n{{field-1}} ${i}^{2})$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235420 :question/problem "The exact value of the area $A$ from part 7 can be expressed using definite integral notation as\n\n$A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235026 :answer-field/correct "answer-c590c56f-2ce8-56b4-a40f-e206402a78d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c590c56f-2ce8-56b4-a40f-e206402a78d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c590c56f-2ce8-56b4-a40f-e206402a78d4" :answer/id #uuid "c590c56f-2ce8-56b4-a40f-e206402a78d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4-x^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110766 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-66ea6fa9-fa1e-5e19-8e8b-006a3addc0db" "question-0b7d3007-c733-562f-b061-3d6372ea77d3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110395 :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle DAB$ and $m\\angle DAB = 79^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle DAC?$\n\n![](images/8d/8d02999cf6ae7ec69fee2cdac99b8dc214c59f1ed50bbf85bec6bec01f2212ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "An angle bisector divides the angle into two congruent angles with equal measures, so $m\\angle BAC = m\\angle DAC .$\n\n![](images/6e/6e7c8ce2713441e633d7ecc3548c504d2522b4771eb6355217d6ab766a647b67.png)\n\nEach of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DAC & =\\frac{m∠DAB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{79}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={39.5}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110767 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9a317f88-19a8-56a2-bc2b-47dd254aaf3c" "question-c102a149-79c1-57ef-8166-900c7a1db3c0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110397 :question/problem "In the figure below, ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/cf/cf8d89fc393869edb95a2fdb8b75d24bb04f3de676fd73cdd9d8a19af092a00a.svg) What is the measure of ![](images/8d/8d4751af85bf1737162fafc15e01e261b992f958ee7be84e5b03a2c7dcfedfbf.svg)\n\n![](images/91/910939d11fc9c0acdb5fbb7e073b67c8fcbf91ea5a88e74851f0f5f900f5123a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0d040e66003036f8fa31da39da08d78506511f501b1a3f5cd1f39efe16f910c.svg) are supplementary, we have \n\n![](images/47/47f63dede9eaaa0f1ed64bc2cc6110bdbab7f3d52052201284417d1bf7336e5a.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/64/6450e078b6f1dec407838cfd67a8ec79507bc9ffa00ab12fc167d6087dc8cfe4.svg) is the bisector of ![](images/f5/f5f6b946618b35ae916b01cb82e1a5e741f41993f303f02b5c119351d7452346.svg), we obtain\n\n![](images/4b/4b9eaaf39d74d934992b68c439bff0bed5c7ef5fa1f0d59620993caed61f0860.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110769 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8c39734e-60e8-53eb-a61a-e2f6712a7e91" "question-e88bb8a0-0e79-51e0-a5d2-1fbecece5fbd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110398 :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ is the bisector of $\\angle AOB.$ If $m \\angle AOD = 90^\\circ$ and $m \\angle COD = 35^\\circ,$ find the measure of $\\angle BOD.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b76c6ee6e576fa2701993e941c27f1168c213d9f5f26b1aa8a50759c3711404.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/5a/5ae4873418247e8d825233f903ed359ebee4d2c51ad73e6c7763102a23acded6.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠AOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{35}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOB =2 m \\angle AOC = 110^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, the angle addition postulate gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & =m∠AOB-m∠AOD \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}-{90}^{∘} \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110770 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cee4d62e-6294-5674-b439-787997c480d5" "question-b176a2ae-2130-5324-9a8b-d961a2933f07"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110399 :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC .$ What is the measure of $\\angle GAF?$\n\n![](images/45/458893f38204421326fd7dcb0693b1688eeb61fb3988e00b2d36763b47adc2d2.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/3b/3b0fddd3229947725b0f4f07e58ce9bfac3a9e7e348c25757162f5bc8e03d699.png)\n\nLet $m \\angle FAC=x$ and $m \\angle CAG=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=m\\angle FAC + m \\angle CAG=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BAC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAD=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAC+m∠CAD & =m∠BAD \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m \\angle GAF=x+y=90^\\circ.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-66ea6fa9-fa1e-5e19-8e8b-006a3addc0db" :question/id #uuid "66ea6fa9-fa1e-5e19-8e8b-006a3addc0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-117146" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f818ebe4aea90c33ba1eb3feccf988b0486565da9177df1e98460a950f8b22e.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠AOB.$ If $m∠AOB={58}^{∘}$, find $m∠AOC.$" :question/worked-solution "The bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Each of these congruent angles has a measure that is half the measure of the larger bisected angle. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =\\frac{m∠AOB}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{58}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={29}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0bd8a51-6747-5bfc-b459-183d1248b264"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0bd8a51-6747-5bfc-b459-183d1248b264" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0bd8a51-6747-5bfc-b459-183d1248b264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-94502480-4b15-54ca-9900-12ac33fe1398" :answer-field/choices ["answer-731fc0c1-49c0-5337-8789-e76a292ba140" "answer-095a92cd-dd47-564d-9d40-64701fd71e1b" "answer-0bbda6e3-12f9-5be3-805d-19c2abbdc436" "answer-94502480-4b15-54ca-9900-12ac33fe1398" "answer-eca63fd7-5377-556e-9fe0-f34f11da1ad4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-731fc0c1-49c0-5337-8789-e76a292ba140" :answer/id #uuid "731fc0c1-49c0-5337-8789-e76a292ba140" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{24}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-095a92cd-dd47-564d-9d40-64701fd71e1b" :answer/id #uuid "095a92cd-dd47-564d-9d40-64701fd71e1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bbda6e3-12f9-5be3-805d-19c2abbdc436" :answer/id #uuid "0bbda6e3-12f9-5be3-805d-19c2abbdc436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{27}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94502480-4b15-54ca-9900-12ac33fe1398" :answer/id #uuid "94502480-4b15-54ca-9900-12ac33fe1398" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eca63fd7-5377-556e-9fe0-f34f11da1ad4" :answer/id #uuid "eca63fd7-5377-556e-9fe0-f34f11da1ad4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b7d3007-c733-562f-b061-3d6372ea77d3" :question/id #uuid "0b7d3007-c733-562f-b061-3d6372ea77d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117143" :question/problem "![](images/62/6239a47a2bd88b631f9058c7afe3b59c10b8e71fb8957fe7a354cc7e72daf69d.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠BOC={72}^{∘}$ and $m∠AOC=7x+{9}^{∘}.$ If $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠AOB,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6a/6a49c1d69a34d0159cd24869b51cb7a517021a147e0e831b969c1135f6b533fc.png)\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Thus, $∠AOC≅∠BOC.$ Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠BOC \\\\ 7x+{9}^{∘} & ={72}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={63}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={9}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0e88a87f-5bc0-5820-a8c2-8fb21a9941e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0e88a87f-5bc0-5820-a8c2-8fb21a9941e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e88a87f-5bc0-5820-a8c2-8fb21a9941e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7bdee111-db70-58d2-9682-ad26b784e60c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5da70166-d661-5d68-9b37-57eb151b9a78" "answer-90353fa5-7f89-514b-ab76-2eb5bc33c3ba" "answer-b7b820d6-1e83-5f7b-9a5d-7b829738275d" "answer-5b2bf445-6884-5938-84f2-92ebe3b004cb" "answer-7bdee111-db70-58d2-9682-ad26b784e60c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5da70166-d661-5d68-9b37-57eb151b9a78" :answer/id #uuid "5da70166-d661-5d68-9b37-57eb151b9a78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{13}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90353fa5-7f89-514b-ab76-2eb5bc33c3ba" :answer/id #uuid "90353fa5-7f89-514b-ab76-2eb5bc33c3ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7b820d6-1e83-5f7b-9a5d-7b829738275d" :answer/id #uuid "b7b820d6-1e83-5f7b-9a5d-7b829738275d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{7}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b2bf445-6884-5938-84f2-92ebe3b004cb" :answer/id #uuid "5b2bf445-6884-5938-84f2-92ebe3b004cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{8}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bdee111-db70-58d2-9682-ad26b784e60c" :answer/id #uuid "7bdee111-db70-58d2-9682-ad26b784e60c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{9}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a317f88-19a8-56a2-bc2b-47dd254aaf3c" :question/id #uuid "9a317f88-19a8-56a2-bc2b-47dd254aaf3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-173763" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0662ae0e6957786ab7f3821a932e1f36a1c8ca6e8299709daa5e323601194fa.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle, and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={84}^{∘},$ then $m∠COA=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/5291d267ce1e895cfe75261a36c9dbc660a32935b6858ec8986379ee8c8bb766.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={96}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{96}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}+{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={132}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73740857-daba-5806-a042-08e1179b150d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73740857-daba-5806-a042-08e1179b150d" :answer-field/id #uuid "73740857-daba-5806-a042-08e1179b150d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1e17997f-a493-55a9-a1fe-8d56e06efc3f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e17997f-a493-55a9-a1fe-8d56e06efc3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e17997f-a493-55a9-a1fe-8d56e06efc3f" :answer/id #uuid "1e17997f-a493-55a9-a1fe-8d56e06efc3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "132" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c102a149-79c1-57ef-8166-900c7a1db3c0" :question/id #uuid "c102a149-79c1-57ef-8166-900c7a1db3c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76634" :question/problem "![](images/38/38f49977e7d2f280dec7b433e167b3e081ed10716715e7c9caa669e57fe0701a.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle,  and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={134}^{∘},$ find $m∠COA.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8d/8dfbfa4aaa55e2f8175fe829fccc945d29733a84fc5c1641f099b09d805ada2b.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={46}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{46}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}+{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={157}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ff6924a-93f5-5682-9c89-7cd766ada809"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ff6924a-93f5-5682-9c89-7cd766ada809" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff6924a-93f5-5682-9c89-7cd766ada809" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6ce5961e-51ea-502c-a3cb-8e87aec4d386" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ed48092-8b56-5958-b002-24af91be87f8" "answer-c19dfe82-0c5d-53a9-8feb-9d68319f4e47" "answer-33749aa2-09af-5c2d-b96b-976090bb1b61" "answer-5d0e0449-c2d9-5e5a-938e-2b2dedda00ba" "answer-6ce5961e-51ea-502c-a3cb-8e87aec4d386"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ed48092-8b56-5958-b002-24af91be87f8" :answer/id #uuid "0ed48092-8b56-5958-b002-24af91be87f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{144}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c19dfe82-0c5d-53a9-8feb-9d68319f4e47" :answer/id #uuid "c19dfe82-0c5d-53a9-8feb-9d68319f4e47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{147}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33749aa2-09af-5c2d-b96b-976090bb1b61" :answer/id #uuid "33749aa2-09af-5c2d-b96b-976090bb1b61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{153}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d0e0449-c2d9-5e5a-938e-2b2dedda00ba" :answer/id #uuid "5d0e0449-c2d9-5e5a-938e-2b2dedda00ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{154}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ce5961e-51ea-502c-a3cb-8e87aec4d386" :answer/id #uuid "6ce5961e-51ea-502c-a3cb-8e87aec4d386" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{157}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c39734e-60e8-53eb-a61a-e2f6712a7e91" :question/id #uuid "8c39734e-60e8-53eb-a61a-e2f6712a7e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-76640" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7ba1daa74999732ba112150ad8ecb0958f80057a4aeb8ff52c96bf9b44a8337c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC.$ If $m∠BOA={131}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOC={73}^{∘},$ find $m∠DOA.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/68/68c84f70ff4fcd384c21414958ea696469ac6c64749d31c7db7f8792599d77ce.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOC \\\\  & ={131}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={58}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =\\frac{m∠COA}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{58}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={29}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4bd564ea-42d3-5ae4-839f-5f3370875152"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4bd564ea-42d3-5ae4-839f-5f3370875152" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd564ea-42d3-5ae4-839f-5f3370875152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7bf873ed-3ddb-528f-bd21-acec47a39882" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5157657c-e339-58bf-a4e5-8e82ad853592" "answer-f33e3e77-56b9-5061-8045-35c0c44ba3bb" "answer-7bf873ed-3ddb-528f-bd21-acec47a39882" "answer-f907ed05-43a0-59a4-b9b8-3c6e5e8236a3" "answer-f3bbdf67-2306-56d3-bb7a-706ea3e24690"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5157657c-e339-58bf-a4e5-8e82ad853592" :answer/id #uuid "5157657c-e339-58bf-a4e5-8e82ad853592" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f33e3e77-56b9-5061-8045-35c0c44ba3bb" :answer/id #uuid "f33e3e77-56b9-5061-8045-35c0c44ba3bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{27}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bf873ed-3ddb-528f-bd21-acec47a39882" :answer/id #uuid "7bf873ed-3ddb-528f-bd21-acec47a39882" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f907ed05-43a0-59a4-b9b8-3c6e5e8236a3" :answer/id #uuid "f907ed05-43a0-59a4-b9b8-3c6e5e8236a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{32}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3bbdf67-2306-56d3-bb7a-706ea3e24690" :answer/id #uuid "f3bbdf67-2306-56d3-bb7a-706ea3e24690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e88bb8a0-0e79-51e0-a5d2-1fbecece5fbd" :question/id #uuid "e88bb8a0-0e79-51e0-a5d2-1fbecece5fbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117261" :question/problem "![](images/23/23794678967f5b2cd1d409f116a9083aa60f942737ad41fa960e91263691f8c7.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠COA.$ If $m∠BOA={127}^{∘}$ and $m∠BOD={75}^{∘},$ find $m∠BOC.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/72/72c0047a8d0b513a5159d31addf0e4c0c2eeefe0e73ceeadf8e154c37f583039.png)\n\nBy the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & =m∠BOA-m∠BOD \\\\  & ={127}^{∘}-{75}^{∘} \\\\  & ={52}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠DOA=m∠COD={52}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =m∠BOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={75}^{∘}-{52}^{∘} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a5434d2b-7760-524c-b9cd-5f9df1cefcab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5434d2b-7760-524c-b9cd-5f9df1cefcab" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5434d2b-7760-524c-b9cd-5f9df1cefcab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f691a25a-c9d1-5a98-b17a-b5e5771466a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f691a25a-c9d1-5a98-b17a-b5e5771466a2" "answer-df5e8c07-4967-569a-a41e-92fd0ecb376a" "answer-6611433c-bc3c-5849-8340-2ff3e742bd49" "answer-3c6f6718-abd0-54bc-8577-dd9269708b63" "answer-9dd8615d-c2fa-5d36-8505-c05c264107fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f691a25a-c9d1-5a98-b17a-b5e5771466a2" :answer/id #uuid "f691a25a-c9d1-5a98-b17a-b5e5771466a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{23}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df5e8c07-4967-569a-a41e-92fd0ecb376a" :answer/id #uuid "df5e8c07-4967-569a-a41e-92fd0ecb376a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{26}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6611433c-bc3c-5849-8340-2ff3e742bd49" :answer/id #uuid "6611433c-bc3c-5849-8340-2ff3e742bd49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c6f6718-abd0-54bc-8577-dd9269708b63" :answer/id #uuid "3c6f6718-abd0-54bc-8577-dd9269708b63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dd8615d-c2fa-5d36-8505-c05c264107fe" :answer/id #uuid "9dd8615d-c2fa-5d36-8505-c05c264107fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{52}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cee4d62e-6294-5674-b439-787997c480d5" :question/id #uuid "cee4d62e-6294-5674-b439-787997c480d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-52777" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd5be67491f109f8041d33768aca872f9dc7242dd1d91bcbfe116a8277f5bf1.png)\n\nIn the diagram above $∠BOA$ is a straight angle. Given that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠COA$ and $∠BOC$, respectively, find $m∠EOD.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/19/19334b594e8567464103bacc4f0978cbbbc664c3cc5a1a6edd5789e822030c4b.png)\n\nLet $m∠COD=x$ and $m∠EOC=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm∠EOD=m∠COD+m∠EOC=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{⟶}{OD}$ is a bisector of $∠COA,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠COA=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{⟶}{OE}$ is a bisector of $∠BOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠BOC=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $∠AOB$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA+m∠BOC & =m∠AOB \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m∠EOD=x+y={90}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e8f30c6-2b3d-5a8b-bcbe-f22b8602f636"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e8f30c6-2b3d-5a8b-bcbe-f22b8602f636" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e8f30c6-2b3d-5a8b-bcbe-f22b8602f636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-44d3c912-8452-5c6a-a7e7-92d51c858804" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca83119f-a6ce-53eb-98c9-eb804eeeb9b3" "answer-3c24ee1b-7520-5096-ab22-3ef897d113ac" "answer-7cb29a8b-7700-5acc-8d15-bd00df2cc0b7" "answer-c27985f4-8af3-51bf-808c-b77637a70153" "answer-44d3c912-8452-5c6a-a7e7-92d51c858804"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca83119f-a6ce-53eb-98c9-eb804eeeb9b3" :answer/id #uuid "ca83119f-a6ce-53eb-98c9-eb804eeeb9b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{104}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c24ee1b-7520-5096-ab22-3ef897d113ac" :answer/id #uuid "3c24ee1b-7520-5096-ab22-3ef897d113ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{116}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cb29a8b-7700-5acc-8d15-bd00df2cc0b7" :answer/id #uuid "7cb29a8b-7700-5acc-8d15-bd00df2cc0b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{72}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c27985f4-8af3-51bf-808c-b77637a70153" :answer/id #uuid "c27985f4-8af3-51bf-808c-b77637a70153" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44d3c912-8452-5c6a-a7e7-92d51c858804" :answer/id #uuid "44d3c912-8452-5c6a-a7e7-92d51c858804" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b176a2ae-2130-5324-9a8b-d961a2933f07" :question/id #uuid "b176a2ae-2130-5324-9a8b-d961a2933f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-76644" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb5fddbf843a89ffbefeaed1976a2bfd159fea9a41665f99aee260f110016e1c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ respectively. If $m∠DOC=x,$ $m∠COE=2x+{2}^{∘},$ and $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ find the value of $m∠BOC.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/484a70ad0c9eeca1ea03db65f5c687074189ac93f9894b64598278dc285d0d83.png)\n\nFirst, let's express the measures of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ in terms of $x.$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOC=2m∠DOC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ is the bisector of $∠BOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠BOC=2m∠COE=4x+{4}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC+m∠BOC & =m∠AOB \\\\ 2x+4x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={126}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={21}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are asked to find $m∠BOC.$ Substituting $x={21}^{∘}$ into the expression for $m∠BOC$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =4({21}^{∘})+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={84}^{∘}+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={88}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5cf32679-392e-572a-a09d-34cd048592df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5cf32679-392e-572a-a09d-34cd048592df" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf32679-392e-572a-a09d-34cd048592df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a1a926d8-2779-5f8e-b1ec-351f4137e51e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7e5316c-1c70-55a9-82c5-9dee5b4c206e" "answer-224af101-a2f4-5dd9-a26c-78344c7d7253" "answer-6eb2d579-1bb9-570b-b005-855502153d2a" "answer-a1a926d8-2779-5f8e-b1ec-351f4137e51e" "answer-75b52ce4-32d0-5e14-acc3-8d7dcec57c63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7e5316c-1c70-55a9-82c5-9dee5b4c206e" :answer/id #uuid "c7e5316c-1c70-55a9-82c5-9dee5b4c206e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-224af101-a2f4-5dd9-a26c-78344c7d7253" :answer/id #uuid "224af101-a2f4-5dd9-a26c-78344c7d7253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6eb2d579-1bb9-570b-b005-855502153d2a" :answer/id #uuid "6eb2d579-1bb9-570b-b005-855502153d2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1a926d8-2779-5f8e-b1ec-351f4137e51e" :answer/id #uuid "a1a926d8-2779-5f8e-b1ec-351f4137e51e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{88}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75b52ce4-32d0-5e14-acc3-8d7dcec57c63" :answer/id #uuid "75b52ce4-32d0-5e14-acc3-8d7dcec57c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{91}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111842 :tutorial/content "An angle **bisector** is a line that divides an angle into two equal parts. \n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of the angle $\\angle BAD.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bf65e915f4fc26b8d87199f46ea0bc514195d857890983d31aa2d73eb38a811.png)\n\nThe resulting angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are congruent (they have the same measure)." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110863 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
